QB.add(45,[{"id":45001,"t":"Scar Formation","s":"A patient's infected wound is slow to settle. Which finding would <b>most</b> directly prevent resolution of inflammation?","o":["Vessels around the wound remain dilated and fluid is not drained away","Macrophages are rapidly removing debris and fluid is being cleared by vessels","The damaged tissue has good regenerative capacity and stem cell reserves","Pathogens have been destroyed rapidly by neutrophils and macrophages","The inflammatory phase was short, with very little damage to tissue"],"e":"Resolution is the restoration of tissue back to normal, as much as possible. Its five conditions are minimal cell death and damage, regenerative capacity, quick destruction of pathogens, quick removal of debris and fluids, and the end of vasodilation. Persisting vasodilation with poor fluid clearance violates two of these. All the other options describe conditions that favour resolution.","w":["Quick debris and fluid removal is a condition that favours resolution.","Regenerative capacity is a condition that favours resolution.","Quick pathogen destruction is a condition that favours resolution.","A short inflammatory phase with minimal damage favours resolution."],"k":"Resolution needs the end of vasodilation and good fluid drainage.","rn":[["","Why does granulation tissue feel sticky?","Why does granulation tissue feel sticky?","Fluid leaks from new vessels"],["","Where does epidermal re-epithelialization start?","Where does epidermal re-epithelialization start?","Proliferative, migrating leading edge; Located away from the wound edge"]]},{"id":45002,"t":"Resolution of Inflammation","s":"A tissue fulfils the condition of having regenerative capacity. Why does this alone not guarantee complete return of function?","o":["It is only one of several conditions, and the rest of resolution must also be satisfied","Regenerative capacity is a feature found only in permanent cells such as cardiac muscle","Regenerative capacity prevents macrophages from removing debris from the site of injury","Regeneration can occur only after the tissue has already been replaced by a mature scar","Regenerative capacity depends entirely on vasodilation continuing without interruption throughout healing"],"e":"Most tissues can rebuild, but regenerative capacity alone does not guarantee full function. It is one of the five conditions for resolution. Damage must also be minimal, pathogens and debris must be cleared quickly, and vasodilation must end. If inflammation does not end, wound healing runs into problems.","w":["Permanent cells have no regenerative capacity.","Macrophages are still required, and regenerative capacity does not stop them.","Scar formation follows repair, rather than being a precondition for regeneration.","Vasodilation must end for the tissue to return to normal."],"k":"Regenerative capacity is necessary but not sufficient for resolution.","rn":[["","What are the 5 conditions for resolution to proceed?","What are the 5 conditions for resolution to proceed?","Minimal cell death, tissue damage; Regenerative capacity of tissue; Quick destruction of pathogens; Quick removal of debris, fluids; End of vasodilation"]]},{"id":45003,"t":"Macrophages and Growth Factors","s":"Which term describes the role of macrophages when they clear debris after inflammation and provide a clean environment for healing?","a":["scavengers","scavenger","scavenging"],"e":"Macrophages are the scavengers, clearing the debris after inflammation. They remove debris by phagocytosis and ingest fluids by pinocytosis. This provides the clean environment that healing needs, which makes them essential cells in injury recovery.","k":"Macrophages are the scavengers of wound healing.","rn":[["","Which cells are known as the scavengers?","Which cells are known as the scavengers?","Macrophages"]]},{"id":45004,"t":"Resolution of Inflammation","s":"A macrophage has grabbed an apoptotic cell with its receptors, and internal messages are telling it to wrap around. What is the very next event?","o":["It swallows the cell whole","It first lets go and recognises the cell","It releases the cell to neighbouring neutrophils","It divides so that both daughters can share the cell","It secretes the cell's contents into the local tissue space"],"e":"The sequence of eating is recognition, binding, signalling and phagocytosis. Binding through receptors sends signals inside the macrophage telling it to wrap around. The final step is to swallow the dying cell whole, enclosing it in a bubble inside the macrophage. Recognition has already happened by this point.","w":["Recognition comes first and is already complete at this stage.","Macrophages are the population responsible for clearing the cell debris themselves.","Macrophage division is not one of the steps in phagocytosis.","The dying cell is enclosed in a bubble rather than being spilled into the tissue."],"k":"Recognition → binding → signalling → phagocytosis."},{"id":45005,"t":"Resolution of Inflammation","s":"A healthy cell and an apoptotic cell sit side by side. Why is only the apoptotic cell recognised by the macrophage through its phosphatidylserine receptors?","o":["Phosphatidylserine has moved to the outside of its membrane","Phosphatidylserine is absent from the membrane of healthy cells","Phosphatidylserine is a protein made only after the nucleus has broken down","Phosphatidylserine is secreted into the blood and binds to the healthy cell's neighbour","Phosphatidylserine is a complement fragment deposited by the classical pathway"],"e":"Phosphatidylserine is a glycerophospholipid that, in healthy cells, is always on the inside of the cell membrane. In apoptotic cells it moves to the outside, where macrophage receptors can find it. It is an example of a death associated molecular pattern. Healthy cells have it too, but out of reach.","w":["Healthy cells do have it, on the inside of the membrane.","It is a glycerophospholipid, which is a type of fat molecule rather than a protein.","It flips to the outside of the membrane of the dying cell and is not secreted into blood.","It is a membrane lipid, not a complement fragment."],"k":"Phosphatidylserine flips outside in apoptosis, so macrophages can find it.","rn":[["","Where is phosphatidylserine found in a healthy cell?","Where is phosphatidylserine found in a healthy cell?","Inside of the cell membrane"],["","What happens to phosphatidylserine in an apoptotic cell?","What happens to phosphatidylserine in an apoptotic cell?","Moves to outer membrane"]]},{"id":45006,"t":"Resolution of Inflammation","s":"Give the abbreviation or full name of the type of signal to which externalised phosphatidylserine belongs.","a":["damp","damps","death associated molecular pattern","death-associated molecular pattern","death associated molecular patterns"],"e":"A signal such as externalised phosphatidylserine is a DAMP, a death associated molecular pattern. Other DAMPs are proteins or carbohydrates. Macrophage receptors recognise these so that they can bind and engulf dying cells.","k":"PtdSer on the outside acts as a DAMP.","rn":[["","Other examples of DAMPs (death associated molecular patterns) besides phosphatidylserine","Other examples of DAMPs (death associated molecular patterns) besides phosphatidylserine","Proteins; Carbohydrates"],["","Phosphatidylserine on the cell surface is an example of what?","Phosphatidylserine on the cell surface is an example of what?","DAMP (death associated molecular pattern)"]]},{"id":45007,"t":"Resolution of Inflammation","s":"How do apoptotic cells attract phagocytes from a distance, before any direct contact has been made?","o":["They release lysophosphatidic acid, a lipid mediator, by chemotaxis","They express selectins that catch phagocytes flowing past in the blood","They release histamine, which directly opens up a path to the cell","They convert fibrinogen to fibrin, which physically drags phagocytes in","They display CD47, which draws phagocytes towards them from a distance"],"e":"Apoptotic cells attract phagocytes by chemotaxis, releasing lysophosphatidic acid, a lipid mediator. This call for help brings the macrophage close enough for recognition and receptor binding. CD47 by contrast is part of the \"don't eat me\" signalling that protects a cell rather than attracting a phagocyte.","w":["Selectins are not how apoptotic cells attract phagocytes.","Histamine is not the attracting signal released by apoptotic cells.","Fibrin is used to join wounds rather than to attract phagocytes.","CD47 and SIRPα are \"don't eat me\" signalling."],"k":"Lysophosphatidic acid from apoptotic cells attracts phagocytes.","rn":[["","How do apoptotic cells attract phagocytes?","How do apoptotic cells attract phagocytes?","Chemotaxis; Release lysophosphatidic acid (a lipid mediator)"]]},{"id":45008,"t":"Resolution of Inflammation","s":"A stressed cell is badly injured but could still recover. Which mechanism protects it from being eaten by a macrophage?","o":["The cell displays a \"don't eat me\" signal, which the macrophage checks before fully engulfing","The cell moves phosphatidylserine to the outside of its membrane to show it is still healthy","The cell releases lysophosphatidic acid to send macrophages away from its neighbourhood","The macrophage will only ever eat necrotic cells and never binds cells with intact membranes","The cell permanently removes all of its membrane receptors until the repair has been completed"],"e":"A cell can be injured yet still able to undergo repair, and may be close to destruction but not ready to die. Such cells display a \"don't eat me\" signal, which macrophages also recognise. Macrophages check for this before fully engulfing and destroying. As a result, cells that can still recover are not eaten by mistake.","w":["Externalised phosphatidylserine marks an apoptotic cell for removal.","Lysophosphatidic acid attracts phagocytes rather than repelling them.","Macrophages eliminate apoptotic as well as necrotic cells.","Loss of membrane receptors is not the protective mechanism described."],"k":"\"Don't eat me\" signals protect recoverable cells.","rn":[["","What do macrophages check for before engulfing an injured cell?","What do macrophages check for before engulfing an injured cell?","A \"don't eat me\" signal"],["","Which cells display a \"don't eat me\" signal?","Which cells display a \"don't eat me\" signal?","Injured but repairable cells"]]},{"id":45009,"t":"Resolution of Inflammation","s":"Which pair of molecules is involved in \"don't eat me\" signalling between a dying cell and a macrophage?","o":["CD47 and SIRPα","TIM4 and BAI1","Stabilin 2 and TIM4","CD300b and BAI1","C1q and C3b"],"e":"CD47 and SIRPα are the labelled \"don't eat me\" signalling pair. TIM4, BAI1, stabilin 2 and CD300b are phosphatidylserine receptors, which promote recognition of apoptotic cells. C1q and C3b are complement factors bound by complement receptors. The point is that macrophages have many receptors, and they weigh signals both for and against eating.","w":["TIM4 and BAI1 are phosphatidylserine receptors that promote recognition.","Stabilin 2 and TIM4 are phosphatidylserine receptors that promote recognition.","CD300b and BAI1 are phosphatidylserine receptors that promote recognition.","C1q and C3b are complement factors bound by complement receptors."],"k":"CD47–SIRPα = \"don't eat me\"; PtdSer receptors promote eating."},{"id":45010,"t":"Macrophages and Growth Factors","s":"Why is the monocyte round while the macrophage is large and spread out?","o":["A round shape suits travel in blood, while a spread-out cell would not be efficient there","A round shape suits phagocytosis in tissues, while a spread-out cell is too small to eat","A monocyte has no nucleus at all, while a macrophage gains its nucleus only once it has settled in the tissue","A monocyte is a type of permanent cell, while a macrophage is a type of adult stem cell","A round shape prevents monocytes from ever leaving the blood to reach the tissues"],"e":"Monocytes are macrophage precursors that are not yet fully differentiated. They circulate in the blood and migrate to tissues, where they differentiate to mature macrophages. A round shape suits travelling in blood, and a macrophage is more spread out, which would not be efficient in blood. Form follows the place where the cell works.","w":["Macrophages are the larger cell and are not too small to eat.","A monocyte has a U-shaped nucleus.","Neither is described as a permanent cell or an adult stem cell here.","Monocytes do migrate from the blood to the tissues."],"k":"Round monocytes travel; spread-out macrophages work in tissues.","rn":[["","Why are monocytes round in the blood?","Why are monocytes round in the blood?","Round shape suits blood circulation; Spread-out form would be less efficient"]]},{"id":45011,"t":"Macrophages and Growth Factors","s":"A student says macrophages only matter when there is inflammation. Which is the best correction?","o":["Cells that need replacing undergo apoptosis in normal life, so resident macrophages clear debris routinely","Macrophages exist only in the blood, and enter tissues only after they have been recruited during an injury","Macrophages appear only after chronic inflammation, because they derive from fibroblasts in old scar tissue","Macrophages have no function at all in normal tissue, and they are only ever found in the lung","Macrophages in healthy tissue are inactive, as chemokines are the only cells that can clear any debris"],"e":"Resident macrophages are fully differentiated macrophages that live in many tissues, ready to clear pathogens and debris after cell death. Debris also comes from normal life, since cells that need replacing undergo apoptosis, and not only during inflammation. So their role is continuous.","w":["Resident macrophages live in many tissues, not only in the blood.","Monocytes, rather than fibroblasts, are the precursors of macrophages.","They live in pretty much all tissues, not just the lung.","Chemokines are messenger molecules rather than cells."],"k":"Resident macrophages clear debris from normal apoptosis as well.","rn":[["","Why do tissues need resident macrophages when there is no infection?","Why do tissues need resident macrophages when there is no infection?","Normal cell turnover (apoptosis) produces debris"]]},{"id":45012,"t":"Tissue Regeneration vs Repair","s":"A liver biopsy contains resident macrophages lining the sinusoids. What are these cells called?","a":["kupffer cells","kupffer cell","kupffer"],"e":"Kupffer cells are the resident macrophages of the liver. The same type of cell has a different local name in each tissue: alveolar macrophages in the lung, microglial cells in the brain, mesangial phagocytes in the kidney and histiocytes in connective tissue. In the regenerating liver, Kupffer cells also release interleukin 6 onto hepatocytes.","k":"Kupffer cells = liver macrophages.","rn":[["","What does the Kupffer cell release in liver regeneration?","What does the Kupffer cell release in liver regeneration?","Release IL-6 (interleukin-6) to prime hepatocytes"],["","Where do Kupffer cells sit?","Where do Kupffer cells sit?","Liver sinusoids"],["","What activates the Kupffer cell in liver regeneration?","What activates the Kupffer cell in liver regeneration?","TNF (tumour necrosis factor)"]]},{"id":45013,"t":"Resolution of Inflammation","s":"Which macrophage population-location pairing is correct?","o":["Microglial cells in the brain and mesangial phagocytes in the kidney","Histiocytes in the brain and microglial cells in connective tissue","Kupffer cells in the spleen and mesangial phagocytes in the liver","Alveolar macrophages in the kidney and osteoclasts in the lung","Synovial A cells in the bone and osteoclasts in the knee joint"],"e":"Microglial cells are in the brain and mesangial phagocytes in the kidney. Histiocytes are in connective tissue, Kupffer cells in the liver, alveolar macrophages in the lung, osteoclasts in bone and synovial A cells in the joints. These are the same type of cell with different local names.","w":["These two pairs have been swapped: histiocytes are in connective tissue and microglial cells in the brain.","Kupffer cells are in the liver, and mesangial phagocytes are in the kidney.","Alveolar macrophages are in the lung, and osteoclasts are in bone.","Synovial A cells are in joints, and osteoclasts are in bone."],"k":"Local names: microglia (brain), mesangial (kidney), Kupffer (liver), alveolar (lung).","rn":[["","How do apoptotic cells attract phagocytes?","How do apoptotic cells attract phagocytes?","Chemotaxis; Release lysophosphatidic acid (a lipid mediator)"]]},{"id":45014,"t":"Macrophages and Growth Factors","s":"A long-term smoker's lung biopsy shows an abundant population of reddish-brown cells packed with ingested material. What is the most likely explanation?","o":["Tissue-resident macrophages have increased in response to more debris","Permanent neurons have migrated into the lung to phagocytose debris","Red blood cells have proliferated and become phagocytes in the alveoli","Fibroblasts have deposited pigment granules along with collagen fibres","Neutrophils have become resident there, living on for several months"],"e":"Alveolar macrophages are tissue-resident macrophages of the lung, and lungs contain a lot of debris. Smokers end up with more debris in their lungs, so they have a more abundant population of these macrophages. They carry ingested debris, which gives the colour seen in the section.","w":["Neurons are permanent cells and do not do this.","Red blood cells are listed as permanent cells and cannot proliferate.","Fibroblasts deposit collagen and are not the cells filled with debris here.","Neutrophils are not the resident cell population described in the lung."],"k":"More lung debris in smokers → more alveolar macrophages.","rn":[["","Why do smokers have more lung resident macrophages?","Why do smokers have more lung resident macrophages?","More debris in their lungs"],["","Image card: Lung tissue: what are the reddish-brown cells?","Lung tissue: what are the reddish-brown cells?","Tissue resident (alveolar) macrophages"]]},{"id":45015,"t":"Macrophages and Growth Factors","s":"During inflammation, vascular permeability leaves a lot of fluid in the tissue. Which macrophage activity deals specifically with this?","o":["Pinocytosis","Phagocytosis","Chemotaxis","Angiogenesis","Haemostasis"],"e":"Macrophage activities are chemotaxis (migration towards a damaged or infected area), phagocytosis (ingesting cell debris) and pinocytosis (ingesting fluids). Vascular permeability during inflammation leaves a lot of fluid, which pinocytosis takes up. Good vascular drainage also helps to remove fluid.","w":["Phagocytosis ingests cell debris and is not specifically for fluid.","Chemotaxis is migration towards a damaged area.","Angiogenesis forms new vessels.","Haemostasis stops bleeding through a clot."],"k":"Move (chemotaxis), eat (phagocytosis), drink (pinocytosis).","rn":[["","Why do macrophages ingest fluid (pinocytosis)?","Why do macrophages ingest fluid (pinocytosis)?","Inflammation leaves much fluid from vascular permeability"],["","What are the 3 macrophage activities?","What are the 3 macrophage activities?","Chemotaxis; Phagocytosis; Pinocytosis"]]},{"id":45016,"t":"Macrophages and Growth Factors","s":"Name the macrophage activity of migrating towards a damaged or infected area in response to chemokines.","a":["chemotaxis"],"e":"Chemotaxis is migration towards a damaged or infected area, and macrophages respond to chemokines. The other two macrophage activities are phagocytosis, which is eating debris, and pinocytosis, which is drinking fluids. All three contribute to cleaning up a wound.","k":"Chemotaxis: migration towards the site, in response to chemokines.","rn":[["","What is meant by Chemotaxis?","What is meant by Chemotaxis?","Migration to damaged/infected area"],["","What are the 3 macrophage activities?","What are the 3 macrophage activities?","Chemotaxis; Phagocytosis; Pinocytosis"]]},{"id":45017,"t":"Macrophages and Growth Factors","s":"Which sequence correctly describes the mechanism of phagocytosis of a microbe?","o":["Recognition and attachment, engulfment into a phagosome, then fusion with a lysosome for killing","Fusion with a lysosome, engulfment into a phagosome, then recognition and attachment of microbe","Engulfment into a lysosome, recognition by a phagosome, then attachment to the plasma membrane","Recognition and attachment, release of enzymes outside the cell, then engulfment of remaining parts","Killing by reactive oxygen species outside of the cell, then engulfment, then attachment of microbe"],"e":"Microbes first bind to phagocyte receptors. The phagocyte membrane then zips up around the microbe, giving a phagosome. A lysosome with enzymes fuses with the phagosome to form a phagolysosome, and lysosomal enzymes degrade the microbe. Reactive oxygen species and nitric oxide also kill ingested microbes.","w":["This puts the stages in reverse order.","The microbe is held in a phagosome, which then fuses with a lysosome.","Enzymes act within the phagolysosome after engulfment.","Reactive oxygen species act in the phagosome after engulfment."],"k":"Attachment → phagosome → phagolysosome → degradation.","rn":[["","What are the 3 stages of phagocytosis of microbes?","What are the 3 stages of phagocytosis of microbes?","Recognition and attachment; Engulfment; Killing and degradation"]]},{"id":45018,"t":"Macrophages and Growth Factors","s":"What is the name of the combined compartment formed by fusion of a phagosome and a lysosome?","a":["phagolysosome","phago-lysosome"],"e":"A phagosome is the bubble holding the swallowed item, and a lysosome is a bag of enzymes that break things down. When they join they form the phagolysosome. Lysosomal enzymes then degrade the contents to basic components such as amino acids, lipids and sugars.","k":"Phagosome + lysosome = phagolysosome.","rn":[["","What forms when phagosome and lysosome fuse?","What forms when phagosome and lysosome fuse?","Phagolysosome"]]},{"id":45019,"t":"Resolution of Inflammation","s":"A macrophage has digested a microbe in a phagolysosome. What happens to the basic components that result?","o":["Amino acids, lipids and sugars are released outside the cell","They are converted into new collagen inside the lysosome itself","They are stored permanently inside the phagosome of the macrophage","They are used to make new phosphatidylserine on the phagocyte's own surface","They are transferred into the nucleus, where they are used as growth factors"],"e":"Lysosomal enzymes break material into basic components, namely amino acids, lipids and sugars. These are released outside the cell after successful phagocytosis. The same process is used to eliminate necrotic cells. The result is a cleaner environment for healing.","w":["Collagen is made by fibroblasts rather than in a macrophage lysosome.","The components are released rather than being stored in a phagosome.","Using them to make surface phosphatidylserine is not the described fate.","Growth factors are signalling molecules and are not made in this way."],"k":"Digestion products (amino acids, lipids, sugars) leave the cell.","rn":[["","How do apoptotic cells attract phagocytes?","How do apoptotic cells attract phagocytes?","Chemotaxis; Release lysophosphatidic acid (a lipid mediator)"]]},{"id":45020,"t":"Macrophages and Growth Factors","s":"Which statement about phagocyte killing chemistry is correct?","o":["Phagocyte oxidase assembles in the phagosome membrane and converts oxygen into superoxide and hydrogen peroxide","Phagocyte oxidase works only in lysosomes and converts arginine into amino acids, lipids and sugars for release outside the cell","Inducible nitric oxide synthase uses oxygen to make hydrogen peroxide inside the nucleus","Myeloperoxidase is found in Kupffer cells and converts hypochlorite back into harmless water","Reactive oxygen species are made outside of the phagocyte by surrounding fibroblasts"],"e":"In activated phagocytes, parts of phagocyte oxidase come together in the phagosome membrane to form the active enzyme. It converts oxygen into superoxide and hydrogen peroxide. Myeloperoxidase in neutrophil granules converts hydrogen peroxide to hypochlorite, causing irreversible damage to the pathogen. Inducible nitric oxide synthase uses arginine to make nitric oxide.","w":["Phagocyte oxidase uses oxygen at the phagosome membrane and not arginine in lysosomes.","Inducible nitric oxide synthase uses arginine to make nitric oxide.","Myeloperoxidase is in neutrophil granules and makes hypochlorite.","Reactive oxygen species are formed in the membrane of phagosomes within the phagocyte."],"k":"Phagocyte oxidase: O2 → superoxide, H2O2; MPO → hypochlorite.","rn":[["","What does phagocyte oxidase convert oxygen into?","What does phagocyte oxidase convert oxygen into?","Superoxide; H2O2 (hydrogen peroxide)"]]},{"id":45021,"t":"Macrophages and Growth Factors","s":"Which enzyme in neutrophil granules converts hydrogen peroxide to hypochlorite, damaging the pathogen irreversibly?","a":["myeloperoxidase","mpo"],"e":"Myeloperoxidase (MPO), in neutrophil granules, converts hydrogen peroxide to hypochlorite. Hypochlorite reacts with bacterial molecules, causing irreversible damage to the pathogen. Hydrogen peroxide itself comes from the action of phagocyte oxidase on oxygen.","k":"MPO: H2O2 → hypochlorite.","rn":[["","What does MPO (myeloperoxidase) do?","What does MPO (myeloperoxidase) do?","Converts H2O2 (hydrogen peroxide) to hypochlorite; Present in neutrophil granules"]]},{"id":45022,"t":"Macrophages and Growth Factors","s":"If a phagocyte's granule contents are released during phagocytosis, where do they go, and what follows from that?","o":["Into extracellular tissues, so that neighbouring tissue is exposed to them","Into the nucleus, so that the macrophage's gene expression shuts down","Into the lumen of the lysosome only, so that they can never leave the phagocyte","Into the blood, where they are converted to chemokines and growth factors","Into the fibrin clot, so that the clot becomes stronger than scar tissue"],"e":"During phagocytosis, granule contents may be released into extracellular tissues, which means outside the cell. Killing chemicals and enzymes are therefore not always contained within the phagosome. This explains why the inflammatory phase needs to cause as little cell death and tissue damage as possible for resolution.","w":["Granule contents do not act in this way on the nucleus.","The contents may be released outside the cell, so they are not always contained.","Conversion to chemokines and growth factors is not described.","Strengthening a clot is not a described effect of granule contents."],"k":"Granule contents can spill into extracellular tissue."},{"id":45023,"t":"Macrophages and Growth Factors","s":"A wound has moved beyond the acute fight and into tissue repair. Which macrophage population and products would you expect to dominate at that point?","o":["M2 macrophages releasing anti-inflammatory cytokines and growth factors","M1 macrophages releasing pro-inflammatory cytokines","M2 macrophages releasing only reactive oxygen species into the wound","M1 macrophages depositing type I collagen across the wound bed","Both types releasing lysophosphatidic acid to attract neutrophils"],"e":"M1 macrophages are generally considered pro-inflammatory and release pro-inflammatory cytokines. M2 macrophages are generally considered anti-inflammatory and are involved in tissue repair and wound healing, releasing anti-inflammatory cytokines and growth factors. Macrophages produce growth factors after their phagocytic function and resolution of inflammation.","w":["M1 macrophages drive the inflammatory response rather than the repair phase.","M2 macrophages are characterised by anti-inflammatory cytokines and growth factors.","Fibroblasts deposit collagen.","Lysophosphatidic acid is released by apoptotic cells."],"k":"M1: pro-inflammatory. M2: anti-inflammatory, repair, growth factors.","rn":[["","What do M2 macrophages release?","What do M2 macrophages release?","Anti-inflammatory cytokines; Growth factors"]]},{"id":45024,"t":"Macrophages and Growth Factors","s":"How do growth factors change the behaviour of a target cell?","o":["Ligand binding triggers phosphorylation and a signalling cascade, which alters gene expression","Ligand entry through the nuclear pore directly rewrites the DNA sequence of the target cell so that it grows more rapidly","Ligand binding digests the receptor, which releases amino acids used in new cell growth","Ligand binding physically splits the cell into two daughter cells without any other signal","Ligand binding blocks all gene expression, so the target cell then becomes quiescent"],"e":"Growth factors are signalling molecules that affect cell growth, proliferation and differentiation through altering gene expression. A ligand binds a receptor, which triggers phosphorylation and a signalling cascade. Gene expression is altered, and the activity of the cell changes. Which instructions the cell uses changes, not the DNA sequence.","w":["Gene expression changes, but the DNA sequence is not rewritten.","The receptor is not digested to provide building blocks.","Binding starts a cascade rather than physically splitting the cell.","Growth factors promote proliferation rather than blocking all gene expression."],"k":"Growth factor → receptor → phosphorylation cascade → gene expression → activity.","rn":[["","How do growth factors alter gene expression?","How do growth factors alter gene expression?","Ligand binds receptor; Phosphorylation; Signalling cascade"]]},{"id":45025,"t":"Resolution of Inflammation","s":"Which option is not named as an example of a growth factor or related molecule acting in tissue repair?","o":["Phosphatidylserine","Fibroblast growth factor","Transforming growth factor","Platelet-derived growth factor","Tumour necrosis factor, which also acts in acute inflammation"],"e":"Examples given are fibroblast growth factor, transforming growth factor, platelet-derived growth factor and tumour necrosis factor. Tumour necrosis factor is also present in the acute inflammatory response and has roles in healing. Phosphatidylserine is a membrane lipid that marks apoptotic cells for macrophage recognition.","w":["Fibroblast growth factor is a named example.","Transforming growth factor is a named example.","Platelet-derived growth factor is a named example.","Tumour necrosis factor is a named example."],"k":"Examples: FGF, TGF, PDGF, TNF.","rn":[["","Which molecule do macrophages recognise on apoptotic cells?","Which molecule do macrophages recognise on apoptotic cells?","Phosphatidylserine (PtdSer)"],["","What happens to phosphatidylserine in an apoptotic cell?","What happens to phosphatidylserine in an apoptotic cell?","Moves to outer membrane"],["","What type of molecule is phosphatidylserine (PtdSer)?","What type of molecule is phosphatidylserine (PtdSer)?","Glycerophospholipid"]]},{"id":45026,"t":"Macrophages and Growth Factors","s":"Which set of target cells do growth factors act on to promote proliferation and differentiation in wound healing?","o":["Epithelial cells, fibroblasts, endothelial cells, and stem or progenitor cells","Red blood cells, neurons, cardiac muscle cells and skeletal muscle cells only","Only neutrophils, and only when they are in the blood rather than the tissue","Only bacteria, in order to reduce the amount of infection in the wound","Only platelets, in order to trigger formation of a new clot across the wound"],"e":"Growth factors act on epithelial cells, fibroblasts, endothelial cells and stem and progenitor cells, helping them proliferate and differentiate. Permanent cells are terminally differentiated and cannot proliferate. So the listed target cells are the ones doing the rebuilding.","w":["These are listed as permanent cells that cannot proliferate.","Neutrophils are not listed as the targets of growth factors.","Growth factors act on the host's cells and do not target bacteria here.","Platelets are not listed as the targets of growth factors."],"k":"Growth factor targets: epithelium, fibroblasts, endothelium, stem/progenitor cells.","rn":[["","Which 4 cell groups do growth factors act on?","Which 4 cell groups do growth factors act on?","Epithelial cells; Fibroblasts; Endothelial cells; Stem and progenitor cells"]]},{"id":45027,"t":"Tissue Regeneration vs Repair","s":"A patient has a shallow abrasion that heals completely, with tissue indistinguishable from normal. Another has a deep wound that heals with connective tissue and only partial function. Which term applies to each?","o":["The first is regeneration; the second is repair","The first is repair; the second is regeneration","Both are regeneration, with different amounts of collagen","Both are repair, because each involved an injury","The first is fibrosis; the second is resolution"],"e":"Regeneration follows small, mild, superficial injuries, with the original tissue filling the gap and complete functionality regained. Repair follows more severe or prolonged injury, with connective tissue patching the gap, a scar forming and only some function restored. The terms are not interchangeable.","w":["This reverses the two definitions.","A connective tissue patch with partial function is repair rather than regeneration.","Return to the normal state with full function is regeneration rather than repair.","Fibrosis is scarring, and neither wound is correctly matched by this pair."],"k":"Regeneration = original tissue, full function. Repair = connective tissue, some function.","rn":[["","Regeneration vs repair: type of injury","Regeneration vs repair: type of injury","Regeneration: small, mild, superficial; Repair: severe, deep"],["","Regeneration vs repair: functionality","Regeneration vs repair: functionality","Regeneration: complete; Repair: some restored"]]},{"id":45028,"t":"Tissue Regeneration vs Repair","s":"Why can a tissue with good regenerative capacity still heal by repair after a very severe or prolonged injury?","o":["Extended destruction of epithelial or endothelial tissue favours repair over regeneration","Very severe injuries always remove every stem cell from the whole body at the same time, leaving none for the wound","Regeneration can occur only in permanent cells, so tissues of this type never regenerate","Scar tissue is stronger than normal tissue, so it is always chosen by the body","Prolonged injury stops macrophages from producing any growth factors for the tissue"],"e":"Tissues with regenerative capacity still undergo repair in very severe or prolonged injury, such as chronic inflammation. Epithelial or endothelial tissue undergoes extended destruction, so too much is lost, favouring repair. An open wound is also an opportunity for microbes to enter the body, so it needs to be covered as soon as possible.","w":["Whole-body loss of all stem cells is not the reason given.","Permanent cells have no regenerative capacity.","Scars reach at most 80% of normal skin strength.","Complete loss of growth factor production is not the reason given."],"k":"Extended destruction favours repair; open wounds must be covered fast.","rn":[["","Why does chronic inflammation favour repair?","Why does chronic inflammation favour repair?","Extended epithelial or endothelial destruction"]]},{"id":45029,"t":"Tissue Regeneration vs Repair","s":"In repeated or severe injury, T lymphocytes and macrophages are active, with interleukin 13 present. Which macrophage products and cells then push the tissue towards fibrosis?","o":["Transforming growth factor beta and matrix metalloproteinases, with myofibroblasts laying down matrix","Fibrin and fibrinogen, with hepatocytes laying down matrix around the blood vessels and across the whole of the wound","Phosphatidylserine and CD47, with neutrophils laying down matrix around the wound","Histamine and bradykinin, with keratinocytes laying down matrix beneath the epidermis","Hypochlorite and hydrogen peroxide, with neurons laying down matrix in the dermis"],"e":"With repeated or severe injury, T lymphocytes and other lymphoid cells and macrophages are involved, with cytokines such as interleukin 13. Macrophage products include transforming growth factor beta and matrix metalloproteinases. Myofibroblasts arise from fibroblast recruitment and differentiation and produce extracellular matrix. The end point is fibrosis.","w":["Fibrin forms a temporary join, and hepatocytes are not the cells that lay down the matrix here.","These are apoptosis and \"don't eat me\" molecules, not fibrosis drivers.","Keratinocytes and these vascular mediators are not the pathway described.","These are killing chemicals, and neurons do not lay down matrix."],"k":"TGF-β, MMPs and myofibroblasts drive fibrosis.","rn":[["","What does TGF stand for?","What does TGF stand for?","Transforming growth factor"],["","What is laid down at the end of the fibrosis pathway?","What is laid down at the end of the fibrosis pathway?","Extracellular matrix"]]},{"id":45030,"t":"Tissue Regeneration vs Repair","s":"A patient has a major heart muscle injury and an equally sized skin injury. Why does the skin have a better capacity to rebuild?","o":["Skin has quiescent adult stem cells, which are not present in heart muscle","Heart muscle has too many adult stem cells, which then block regeneration","Skin has embryonic stem cells only, whereas heart muscle has adult stem cells","Heart muscle lacks macrophages, which are the only source of new tissue","Skin is made entirely of permanent cells, which are able to proliferate rapidly"],"e":"Regeneration relies on adult stem cells held in a quiescent state, which is non-proliferative but available. They are present in intestinal epithelium, skin and bone marrow, and not present in heart muscle or the neural system. They begin to proliferate and differentiate in response to cell signalling.","w":["Adult stem cells are not present in heart muscle.","Regeneration in skin relies on adult stem cells.","Macrophages clear debris and produce growth factors but do not become new tissue.","Permanent cells cannot proliferate."],"k":"Adult stem cells: intestine, skin, bone marrow; not heart muscle or nervous system.","rn":[["","Which 3 tissues have quiescent adult stem cells?","Which 3 tissues have quiescent adult stem cells?","Intestinal epithelium; Skin; Bone marrow"],["","Which 2 tissues lack these stem cells?","Which 2 tissues lack these stem cells?","Heart muscle; Neural system"]]},{"id":45031,"t":"Tissue Regeneration vs Repair","s":"Which term describes the state of adult stem cells that are non-proliferative but available?","a":["quiescent","quiescence","quiescent state"],"e":"Adult stem cells are held in a quiescent state, which means non-proliferative but available, or asleep but on call. They wake and begin to proliferate and differentiate in response to cell signalling during regeneration and repair. Reservoirs of these cells give a tissue its ability to rebuild.","k":"Quiescent = non-proliferative but available.","rn":[["","What is meant by Quiescent?","What is meant by Quiescent?","Non-proliferative"]]},{"id":45032,"t":"Tissue Regeneration vs Repair","s":"An 88-year-old and a 20-year-old have the same shallow skin injury, but the older person heals more slowly. What is the best explanation?","o":["The adult stem cell population diminishes with age, decreasing regenerative and repair capacity","Older people have more stem cells, and these compete with fibroblasts for the wound space","Adult stem cells are switched on only in old age, so regeneration begins later in life","Skin loses its macrophages entirely with age, and so all wounds in older people become ulcers","Scalpel cuts and abrasions heal in the same way at all ages because stem cell number never changes"],"e":"We start with an abundant population of adult stem cells, but it diminishes as we age. So elderly patients have decreased regenerative and repair capacity. Age is therefore listed among the systemic factors that impair wound healing.","w":["The population diminishes rather than increases with age.","The stem cells are available from early life and diminish with age.","Complete loss of skin macrophages with age is not stated.","Age affects the number of stem cells and hence healing capacity."],"k":"Fewer adult stem cells with age → reduced regeneration and repair.","rn":[["","Why do elderly patients regenerate less?","Why do elderly patients regenerate less?","Adult stem cell population diminishes"]]},{"id":45033,"t":"Tissue Regeneration vs Repair","s":"In the skin, in which layer of the epidermis are epithelial stem cells found?","a":["basal cell layer","basal layer","stratum basale","basal"],"e":"In the skin, epithelial stem cells are in the basal cell layer, the deepest layer of the epidermis, sitting on the basal lamina above the connective tissue of the dermis. They replace what is lost in superficial injuries. Basal cells can be seen dividing.","k":"Skin stem cells are in the basal cell layer.","rn":[["","Where in skin are epithelial stem cells found?","Where in skin are epithelial stem cells found?","Basal cell layer"],["","What layer lies between the basal cell layer and the dermis?","What layer lies between the basal cell layer and the dermis?","Basal lamina"]]},{"id":45034,"t":"Tissue Regeneration vs Repair","s":"Which site has the most abundant population of adult stem cells, and also relies on haematopoietic stem cells to make blood cells?","o":["Red bone marrow of spongy bones","Cardiac muscle","Basal layer of the skin","Neural system of the brain","Intestinal surface epithelium"],"e":"The red spongy centre of bone holds the most abundant population of adult stem cells. Bone marrow is also the site of blood cell production, relying on haematopoietic stem cells. Skin and intestinal epithelium have stem cells too, whereas heart muscle and the neural system do not.","w":["Adult stem cells are not present in heart muscle.","Skin has epithelial stem cells, but the bone marrow is the most abundant site.","Adult stem cells are not present in the neural system.","The intestine has stem cells, but bone marrow is the most abundant site."],"k":"Red bone marrow: most abundant adult stem cells.","rn":[["","Image card: Which stem cell site is shown in this bone?","Which stem cell site is shown in this bone?","Red bone marrow; Spongy bone"]]},{"id":45035,"t":"Tissue Regeneration vs Repair","s":"Why does surgery use a scalpel, as far as healing is concerned?","o":["A clean precise cut with aligned sides favours regeneration over repair","A clean precise cut with aligned sides favours granulation over regeneration","A jagged cut brings stem cells to the surface faster, so scalpels only slow it","A scalpel removes a portion of tissue, which is what second intention needs","A scalpel kills all local macrophages, so there is no inflammation at all"],"e":"Skin healing by first intention occurs in minor injuries such as a scalpel incision. If the two sides are aligned close together, healing proceeds quickly and easily. It needs a superficial injury with not many layers affected and a clean precise cut. That is why surgery uses scalpels: to favour regeneration over repair.","w":["Granulation tissue is the key feature of second intention healing.","A clean cut, rather than a jagged one, is what healing by first intention needs.","Second intention is healing where tissue is lost, which scalpels aim to avoid.","Macrophages are not killed by a scalpel, and they are still needed in healing."],"k":"Clean, aligned cut → first intention → regeneration.","rn":[["","Why are scalpels used in surgery?","Why are scalpels used in surgery?","Precise, clean cut; sides align; Favours regeneration over repair"]]},{"id":45036,"t":"Tissue Regeneration vs Repair","s":"What is the correct order of events in skin healing by first intention?","o":["Incision, exudation of fibrinogen forming a weak fibrin join, then collagen synthesis forming a strong join","Incision, collagen synthesis forming a strong join, then fibrinogen forming a weak join on top of it","Incision, granulation tissue filling a large crater, then scar contraction forming a narrow wedge of collagen in the dermis","Exudation of collagen, formation of a strong join, then incision through the mature fibrous scar","Incision, haematoma formation, then callus, followed by lamellar replacement of woven tissue"],"e":"After the incision, fibrinogen from the blood exudes and forms a fibrin network through coagulation, joining the two sides with a weak join. Fibrin is then replaced by collagen synthesised in the connective tissue, giving a strong collagen join, with regrowth of epithelial cells on top. The epidermis becomes continuous again.","w":["The weak fibrin join comes before the strong collagen join.","Granulation tissue in a large crater describes second intention.","The incision comes first, and the initial join is fibrin rather than collagen.","Haematoma and callus are stages of fracture healing."],"k":"First intention: incision → weak fibrin join → strong collagen join.","rn":[["","What are the 3 steps of first intention healing?","What are the 3 steps of first intention healing?","Exudation of fibrinogen; Weak fibrin join; Strong collagen join, epidermal regrowth"]]},{"id":45037,"t":"Tissue Regeneration vs Repair","s":"Which plasma-derived protein leaks into a fresh incision, and is converted into the network that forms the first, weak join?","a":["fibrinogen"],"e":"Fibrinogen, a protein from the blood, exudes into the incision. It is converted to fibrin following activation of the coagulation cascade, forming a fibrin network joining the two sides. This join is weak, like temporary glue. Fibrin is later replaced by collagen.","k":"Fibrinogen → fibrin join (weak) → collagen join (strong).","rn":[["","What are the 3 steps of first intention healing?","What are the 3 steps of first intention healing?","Exudation of fibrinogen; Weak fibrin join; Strong collagen join, epidermal regrowth"]]},{"id":45038,"t":"Tissue Regeneration vs Repair","s":"After partial hepatectomy, with liver architecture preserved, which sequence of signals drives regeneration?","o":["TNF and IL-6 prime hepatocytes, then growth factors such as HGF drive proliferation","HGF primes hepatocytes, then TNF and IL-6 stop all proliferation in hepatocytes","Collagen primes hepatocytes, then fibroblasts differentiate to produce new hepatocytes","IL-13 primes hepatocytes, then matrix metalloproteinases return them to quiescence","EGF primes Kupffer cells, then they divide and become mature new hepatocytes"],"e":"Tumour necrosis factor acts on a Kupffer cell, which releases interleukin 6 onto hepatocytes as priming, moving them from G0 to G1. Epidermal growth factor and transforming growth factor alpha bind EGFR, and hepatocyte growth factor binds MET, driving proliferation. So priming comes first, then growth factor-induced proliferation.","w":["Priming is by TNF and IL-6, and HGF drives proliferation.","The liver regenerates by proliferation of surviving cells.","IL-13 and MMPs are in the fibrosis pathway rather than liver regeneration.","Kupffer cells release IL-6; they do not become hepatocytes."],"k":"Liver: TNF/IL-6 prime (G0→G1), EGF/TGF-α/HGF drive proliferation.","rn":[["","Which growth factors act on hepatocytes after priming?","Which growth factors act on hepatocytes after priming?","EGF (epidermal growth factor); TGF-α; HGF"]]},{"id":45039,"t":"Tissue Regeneration vs Repair","s":"Which pairing of growth factor and receptor is correct in the regenerating liver?","o":["Hepatocyte growth factor with MET","Epidermal growth factor with MET","Hepatocyte growth factor with EGFR","Tumour necrosis factor with MET","Transforming growth factor alpha with MET"],"e":"Epidermal growth factor and transforming growth factor alpha bind EGFR. Hepatocyte growth factor binds MET, the hepatocyte growth factor receptor. Both pathways lead to cell proliferation, with two cells becoming four. Tumour necrosis factor acts earlier on Kupffer cells in priming.","w":["Epidermal growth factor binds EGFR.","Hepatocyte growth factor binds MET.","Tumour necrosis factor acts on Kupffer cells in priming.","Transforming growth factor alpha binds EGFR."],"k":"EGF and TGF-α bind EGFR; HGF binds MET.","rn":[["","What is MET?","What is MET?","Hepatocyte growth factor receptor"],["","Which growth factor binds MET?","Which growth factor binds MET?","HGF"]]},{"id":45040,"t":"Tissue Regeneration vs Repair","s":"A drug prevents Kupffer cells from releasing interleukin 6 after a partial hepatectomy. Which step is most directly impaired?","o":["Priming of hepatocytes from G0 to G1","Return of hepatocytes to quiescence","Binding of hepatocyte growth factor to MET","Conversion of fibrinogen to fibrin","Deposition of scar tissue in the portal area"],"e":"Tumour necrosis factor acts on Kupffer cells in the sinusoid. The Kupffer cells release interleukin 6 onto hepatocytes, which is the priming step, moving hepatocytes from G0 to G1. Growth factors then drive proliferation. The drug therefore directly impairs priming.","w":["Return to quiescence involves anti-proliferative cytokines after liver mass is restored.","This binding occurs in the later stage, and the blocked step is earlier.","Fibrin formation is part of coagulation and is not this signal.","Scar deposition is not the direct result of blocking interleukin 6 release."],"k":"Kupffer IL-6 primes hepatocytes.","rn":[["","What cell cycle transition do primed hepatocytes make?","What cell cycle transition do primed hepatocytes make?","G0 to G1"]]},{"id":45041,"t":"Tissue Regeneration vs Repair","s":"Why does a regenerating liver stop growing when its mass has been restored?","o":["Anti-proliferative cytokines are released and hepatocytes return to quiescence","All hepatocytes die by apoptosis and are replaced by quiescent stem cells","The liver runs out of blood supply, so it has no further growth factors","Scar tissue wraps the liver and physically stops any further growth","Hepatocytes are permanent cells that can divide only a few times"],"e":"Once liver mass is restored, proliferation ends because anti-proliferative cytokines are released and hepatocytes return to quiescence. Otherwise the liver would keep growing. Cytokines thus prime the hepatocytes, growth factors make them divide, and cytokines stop them.","w":["Mass apoptosis of all hepatocytes is not how the process stops.","Running out of blood supply is not given as the mechanism.","Scar wrapping describes cirrhosis rather than normal stopping.","Hepatocytes are stable cells, not permanent cells."],"k":"Anti-proliferative cytokines return hepatocytes to quiescence.","rn":[["","How does liver regeneration end?","How does liver regeneration end?","Anti-proliferative cytokines released; Hepatocytes return to quiescence"]]},{"id":45042,"t":"Tissue Regeneration vs Repair","s":"Besides quiescent hepatocytes, where are progenitor cells thought to be located in the liver?","a":["canals of hering","canal of hering"],"e":"Besides quiescent hepatocytes, the liver is thought to contain progenitor cells in the canals of Hering. The main route of liver regeneration, however, is proliferation of surviving hepatocytes in the stages of priming and growth factor-induced proliferation.","k":"Liver progenitors: canals of Hering.","rn":[["","What progenitor cells does the liver contain?","What progenitor cells does the liver contain?","Canals of Hering"]]},{"id":45043,"t":"Tissue Repair","s":"Which single factor decides whether a damaged liver regenerates or develops cirrhosis?","o":["Whether liver architecture has been preserved or destroyed","Whether its hepatocytes are stable or permanent cells","Whether its Kupffer cells produce any growth factors","Whether the patient has more than one lobe in the liver","Whether fibroblasts have been mobilised from the bone marrow"],"e":"Hepatocytes are stable cells with good regenerative capacity. Regeneration follows when the injury does not affect the organ's architecture. When damage destroys liver architecture, full regeneration is not possible, and conditions such as liver cirrhosis arise, with fibrous scarring and regenerative nodules.","w":["Hepatocytes are stable cells in every case.","Kupffer cell output is part of priming and is not the deciding factor stated.","The number of lobes is not mentioned as a factor.","Fibroblast origin from bone marrow is not the deciding factor stated."],"k":"Liver: architecture intact → regeneration; destroyed → cirrhosis.","rn":[["","Which condition arises when damage destroys liver architecture?","Which condition arises when damage destroys liver architecture?","Liver cirrhosis"],["","Why can a liver with good regenerative capacity still repair instead?","Why can a liver with good regenerative capacity still repair instead?","Tissue architecture destroyed"]]},{"id":45044,"t":"Tissue Repair","s":"On gross examination a liver is small, brown and lumpy across its surface. Under the microscope there are fibrous bands and rounded, distorted units instead of hexagons. What has occurred?","o":["Cirrhosis with fibrous scarring and regenerative nodules","Normal regeneration with restored hepatocyte hexagons","Priming of hepatocytes with no change in architecture","A keloid growing beyond its boundary across the liver","A haematoma forming a callus inside of the liver tissue"],"e":"Normal liver is smooth, shiny and red-pink, with neat hexagonal units. In cirrhosis the liver is smaller, brown and lumpy, with fibrous scarring and regenerative nodules, which are rounded and distorted units. This arises when damage destroys the liver's architecture, so that full regeneration is not possible.","w":["Normal regeneration would restore the architecture.","Priming occurs at a cell cycle level with architecture preserved.","A keloid is an excessive skin scar.","Haematoma and callus are stages of fracture healing."],"k":"Cirrhosis: small, lumpy liver; fibrous bands; regenerative nodules.","rn":[["","What 2 features characterise liver tissue repair?","What 2 features characterise liver tissue repair?","Fibrous scarring; Regenerative nodules"]]},{"id":45045,"t":"Tissue Repair","s":"A patient has just fractured their tibia. Which sequence describes healing correctly?","o":["Haematoma, removal of necrotic tissue, callus, lamellar replacement of woven bone, remodelling","Callus, haematoma, remodelling, removal of necrotic tissue, lamellar replacement of woven bone","Removal of necrotic tissue, remodelling, callus, haematoma, lamellar replacement of woven bone","Lamellar replacement of woven bone, callus, haematoma, removal of necrotic tissue, remodelling","Haematoma, remodelling, lamellar replacement of woven bone, callus, removal of necrotic tissue"],"e":"A haematoma forms immediately and provides the initial structure for repair. Necrotic tissue is removed by macrophages. Callus forms as the first new bone, containing bone with no structure and cartilage. More structural bone progressively replaces it, lamellar replacing woven, and remodelling depends on the mechanical pressures of motion.","w":["The haematoma comes before callus.","The haematoma is the first step.","Lamellar replacement comes after callus.","Remodelling is the last step."],"k":"Fracture: haematoma → debris removal → callus → lamellar bone → remodelling.","rn":[["","In bone repair, what replaces woven bone?","In bone repair, what replaces woven bone?","Lamellar bone"]]},{"id":45046,"t":"Tissue Repair","s":"What is the name of the first new bone at a fracture, which contains bone with no structure and cartilage?","a":["callus","bony callus","fracture callus"],"e":"The callus is the first new bone, containing bone with no structure and cartilage, like a rough first draft. More structural bone progressively replaces the callus, with lamellar bone replacing woven bone. Remodelling then depends on motion.","k":"Callus = first new bone: unstructured bone and cartilage.","rn":[["","What does callus contain?","What does callus contain?","Bone with no structure; Cartilage"],["","What replaces callus in bone repair?","What replaces callus in bone repair?","More structural bone, progressively"]]},{"id":45047,"t":"Tissue Regeneration vs Repair","s":"Why is bone healing classified as repair rather than regeneration?","o":["Bone is connective tissue, and repair is characterised by connective tissue deposition","Bone has no blood supply at all, which makes regeneration impossible in any bone","Bone is made of permanent cells that have lost all ability to proliferate","Bone heals only with a keloid, which by definition is an excessive scar","Bone cannot form a haematoma, so healing does not involve any clot or debris"],"e":"Bone is connective tissue, and repair is characterised by deposition of connective tissue. So bone heals by repair. The steps run from haematoma through debris removal, callus and structural bone, to remodelling. Remodelling depends on motion, which is why following advice in recovery matters.","w":["A blood clot, the haematoma, forms immediately at the site.","Bone is not listed among the permanent cell examples.","A keloid is an excessive skin scar.","A haematoma forms immediately and provides the initial structure."],"k":"Bone = connective tissue → heals by repair.","rn":[["","What is tissue repair characterised by?","What is tissue repair characterised by?","Scar formation; Deposition of connective tissue"]]},{"id":45048,"t":"Tissue Repair","s":"A patient with a healed fracture ignores advice and keeps the limb completely unused for a long time. Which part of fracture healing is most directly affected by this behaviour?","o":["Remodelling, because it depends on the mechanical pressures of motion","Haematoma formation, because the clot needs daily movement to form","Callus formation, because the callus is produced only by muscle contraction","Macrophage removal of debris, because the macrophages move only with exercise","Priming of bone cells, which moves them from G0 to G1 through interleukin 6"],"e":"Remodelling, the reorganisation of bone, depends on the mechanical pressures of motion. That is why it is important to follow the doctor's advice during recovery from a fracture. The haematoma, callus and debris removal occur earlier and are not described as motion-dependent.","w":["The haematoma forms immediately after the fracture.","Callus is not described as being produced by muscle contraction.","Macrophage clearance is not described as dependent on exercise.","This describes priming in liver regeneration rather than bone."],"k":"Bone remodelling depends on motion, so follow recovery advice.","rn":[["","What does bone remodelling depend on?","What does bone remodelling depend on?","Mechanical pressures of motion"]]},{"id":45049,"t":"Tissue Repair","s":"Which option lists only permanent cells?","o":["Neurons and cardiac muscle","Hepatocytes and neurons","Hepatocytes and intestinal epithelium","Bone marrow stem cells and cardiac muscle","Basal skin stem cells and skeletal muscle"],"e":"Permanent cells have no regenerative capacity. They are terminally differentiated and cannot proliferate. The listed examples are red blood cells, keratinocytes, neurons, cardiac and skeletal muscle. Hepatocytes are stable cells and stem cells are by nature capable of proliferating.","w":["Hepatocytes are stable cells with good regenerative capacity.","Neither hepatocytes nor intestinal epithelium are permanent cells.","Bone marrow stem cells are a reservoir of regenerative capacity.","Basal skin stem cells proliferate and differentiate."],"k":"Permanent: red cells, keratinocytes, neurons, cardiac and skeletal muscle.","rn":[["","Examples of permanent cells","Examples of permanent cells","Red blood cells; Keratinocytes; Neurons; Cardiac and skeletal muscle"]]},{"id":45050,"t":"Macrophages and Growth Factors","s":"Skeletal muscle has resident progenitor cells. How does its regenerative capacity compare with skin?","o":["Very decreased, even with those progenitor cells present","Far greater, since progenitors make it the fastest-healing tissue","Identical, since both tissues have equal adult stem cell reservoirs","Absent, because skeletal muscle has no progenitor cells of any kind","Greater in scar formation, because it can make a keloid only in muscle"],"e":"Skeletal muscle has resident progenitor cells, but its regenerative capacity is very decreased compared with skin. Skeletal muscle is therefore included among the permanent cell examples. This is a detail that distinguishes it from skin, where adult stem cells replace what is lost in superficial injuries.","w":["Regenerative capacity is decreased rather than greater.","Skin has much better regenerative capacity.","It does have resident progenitor cells.","Keloids are not described as a feature of skeletal muscle."],"k":"Skeletal muscle progenitors exist, but capacity is very decreased vs skin.","rn":[["","Which 4 cell groups do growth factors act on?","Which 4 cell groups do growth factors act on?","Epithelial cells; Fibroblasts; Endothelial cells; Stem and progenitor cells"],["","What progenitor cells does the liver contain?","What progenitor cells does the liver contain?","Canals of Hering"]]},{"id":45051,"t":"Tissue Repair","s":"What term describes cells that are fully specialised, in their final form, and so unable to proliferate?","a":["terminally differentiated","terminal differentiation","permanent cells","permanent"],"e":"Permanent cells are terminally differentiated, meaning fully specialised and in their final form, and they cannot proliferate. They lack the adult stem cells that other tissues rely on, so they have no regenerative capacity. Examples include neurons and cardiac muscle.","k":"Terminally differentiated cells cannot proliferate.","rn":[["","What are permanent cells?","What are permanent cells?","Terminally differentiated; Cannot proliferate; No regenerative capacity"]]},{"id":45052,"t":"Tissue Repair","s":"A surgeon asks where new vessels in a healing wound come from. Which is the best answer?","o":["They sprout from existing vessels, driven by proliferating endothelial cells","They are made from scratch by fibroblasts in the wound bed","They form when neutrophils line up and differentiate into endothelium","They are derived from collagen fibres that hollow out to form a lumen","They are made by macrophages fusing to form tubes in the wound"],"e":"Angiogenesis is new vessel formation after tissue injury. New vessels form from existing vessels and cannot be built from scratch. Vascular endothelial cells receive signalling molecules and drive formation through proliferation. Extracellular matrix proteins provide a scaffold for this.","w":["New vessels are formed from existing vessels and not from scratch.","Neutrophils are not the cells that form the vessel.","Collagen and matrix give a scaffold but do not become the vessel.","Macrophages are not described as fusing to form vessels."],"k":"New vessels sprout from existing vessels.","rn":[["","Which cells drive new vessel formation through proliferation?","Which cells drive new vessel formation through proliferation?","Vascular endothelial cells"],["","What do new blood vessels form from?","What do new blood vessels form from?","Existing vessels"]]},{"id":45053,"t":"Tissue Repair","s":"Which molecule pairing is correct for the first stage of a new vessel sprout?","o":["Vascular endothelial growth factor with vasodilation, angiopoietin with pericyte detachment","Angiopoietin with vasodilation, vascular endothelial growth factor with pericyte detachment from the vessel wall","Matrix metalloproteinases with vasodilation, Notch with basement membrane degradation","Matrix metalloproteinases with pericyte detachment, angiopoietin with tip cell selection","Notch with pericyte detachment, angiopoietin with basement membrane degradation"],"e":"Under angiogenic factors, vasodilation is mediated by vascular endothelial growth factor. Pericyte detachment is mediated by angiopoietin. Basement membrane degradation is mediated by matrix metalloproteinases. A leading tip cell, under vascular endothelial growth factor and Notch signals, pushes outward.","w":["These two have been swapped.","Matrix metalloproteinases degrade basement membrane, and Notch is a tip cell signal.","Angiopoietin is linked to pericyte detachment and matrix metalloproteinases to the membrane.","Notch is a tip cell signal, and matrix metalloproteinases degrade basement membrane."],"k":"VEGF: dilation; angiopoietin: pericyte detachment; MMPs: membrane degradation.","rn":[["","What does VEGF stand for?","What does VEGF stand for?","Vascular endothelial growth factor"],["","What causes pericyte detachment?","What causes pericyte detachment?","Angiopoietin"]]},{"id":45054,"t":"Tissue Repair","s":"Why would blocking extracellular matrix deposition impair formation of a new vessel, even if endothelial cells are proliferating well?","o":["Matrix proteins provide the scaffold that sprouts need to elongate and connect","Matrix proteins are themselves the source of the vascular endothelial cells that line the new vessel","Matrix proteins are the only signals that can make the pericytes detach","Matrix proteins convert fibrinogen into fibrin along the new vessel's lumen","Matrix proteins keep the existing vessels quiescent, stopping sprouting"],"e":"Extracellular matrix proteins provide a scaffold for the proliferation of endothelial cells and formation of the new vessel, in vascular remodelling. Sprouts from two vessels meet in the middle with elongation of the vascular stalk and pericyte recruitment. They need this matrix, and cannot simply sit next to each other.","w":["Matrix proteins do not turn into endothelial cells.","Pericyte detachment is linked to angiopoietin.","Fibrin conversion follows activation of the coagulation cascade.","Sprouting needs matrix as a scaffold, which is not a way of keeping vessels quiescent."],"k":"ECM scaffold is required for new vessel formation.","rn":[["","What provides a scaffold for endothelial proliferation?","What provides a scaffold for endothelial proliferation?","Extracellular matrix proteins"]]},{"id":45055,"t":"Tissue Repair","s":"Name the cell on the outside of a capillary that detaches when an angiogenic sprout starts and is recruited back during elongation.","a":["pericyte","pericytes"],"e":"A pericyte is a cell on the outside of the vessel. Under angiopoietin, pericytes detach as a sprout starts. During elongation of the vascular stalk, there is pericyte recruitment. The result is a complete channel with an open lumen connecting two vessels.","k":"Pericytes detach, then are recruited during elongation.","rn":[["","What happens after pericyte detachment in angiogenesis?","What happens after pericyte detachment in angiogenesis?","Pericytes are recruited; ECM (extracellular matrix) forms between sprouts"],["","What causes pericyte detachment?","What causes pericyte detachment?","Angiopoietin"]]},{"id":45056,"t":"Scar Formation","s":"Scar formation follows the resolution of inflammation. What must come first before fibroblasts can be recruited and form a scar?","o":["Removal of immune cell debris, followed by cell proliferation and angiogenesis","Complete absence of fibroblasts, followed by regeneration of the original tissue","Formation of a mature type I collagen scar, followed by new debris being produced","Permanent vasodilation, followed by persistent accumulation of inflammatory fluid","Continuous bleeding, followed by a long period of unresolved infection in the wound"],"e":"Scar formation is repair involving deposition of connective tissue. It follows the resolution of inflammation and removal of immune cell debris. Cell proliferation and angiogenesis follow, with recruitment of fibroblasts and formation of a scar. It differs from regeneration because collagen is replaced and contracts.","w":["Fibroblasts are needed to form the scar.","Mature type I collagen comes at the end, with remodelling.","Vasodilation must end for resolution.","Continued bleeding or infection interferes with healing rather than leading to a scar."],"k":"Scar: debris clearance → proliferation and angiogenesis → fibroblasts and collagen.","rn":[["","What follows debris removal in scar formation?","What follows debris removal in scar formation?","Cell proliferation; Angiogenesis; Fibroblast recruitment"],["","What does scar formation follow?","What does scar formation follow?","Resolution of inflammation; Removal of immune cell debris"]]},{"id":45057,"t":"Scar Formation","s":"A patient has a large infected wound in which a portion of tissue has been lost, so the two sides cannot be brought together. Which outcome is most likely?","o":["Second intention healing with scar formation and fibrosis","First intention healing with a strong collagen join in days","Complete regeneration without any trace of connective tissue","Quiescence of the wound with no healing process taking place","Formation of a callus, as this is how large skin wounds heal"],"e":"Skin wound healing by second intention occurs in large injuries, for example with continuous blood loss or infection, where a portion of tissue is lost and the two sides cannot align. Regeneration is not possible, so healing goes via repair. Scar formation and fibrosis are the most likely outcome.","w":["First intention needs a minor injury with aligned sides.","Regeneration is not possible where the sides cannot align and tissue is lost.","Healing does occur, by repair.","Callus is a stage in the repair of bone rather than skin."],"k":"Tissue lost, sides can't align → second intention → scar and fibrosis.","rn":[["","Most likely outcome of second intention healing","Most likely outcome of second intention healing","Scar formation; Fibrosis"]]},{"id":45058,"t":"Scar Formation","s":"What is the correct order of the five steps of healing by second intention?","o":["Phagocytosis, granulation, fibroblast proliferation with matrix deposition, epithelial proliferation, remodelling","Granulation, phagocytosis, remodelling, epithelial proliferation, fibroblast proliferation with matrix deposition","Remodelling, phagocytosis, granulation, epithelial proliferation, fibroblast proliferation with matrix deposition","Phagocytosis, epithelial proliferation, remodelling, granulation, fibroblast proliferation with matrix deposition","Epithelial proliferation, fibroblast proliferation with matrix deposition, phagocytosis, granulation, remodelling"],"e":"Phagocytosis comes first, and can make the gap bigger. Granulation follows, with angiogenesis and fibroblast migration. Fibroblasts then proliferate and deposit extracellular matrix. The connective tissue then supports epithelial cells growing over the top. Finally, remodelling leads to scar contraction.","w":["Phagocytosis comes before granulation.","Remodelling is the last step.","Epithelial proliferation needs connective tissue support underneath.","Clean-up by phagocytosis comes first."],"k":"Second intention: phagocytosis, granulation, matrix, epithelium, remodelling.","rn":[["","What 3 things characterise granulation tissue?","What 3 things characterise granulation tissue?","Angiogenesis; Proliferation of fibroblasts; Remaining inflammatory cells"],["","What structure is remodelled in the remodelling phase?","What structure is remodelled in the remodelling phase?","Extracellular matrix"],["","What follows debris removal in scar formation?","What follows debris removal in scar formation?","Cell proliferation; Angiogenesis; Fibroblast recruitment"]]},{"id":45059,"t":"Scar Formation","s":"Why does the epidermis grow across a large wound only after connective tissue has filled the gap?","o":["The connective tissue gives the epithelial cells support to grow on, like a floor","The epidermis is a permanent tissue and cannot grow at all until it is signalled","The connective tissue actively pushes the epidermis down to the base of the wound","The capillary loops consume all growth factors until collagen has been deposited across the wound","The macrophages form a new epithelium that only needs a fibrin clot to begin"],"e":"Collagen fills the gap in the wound and the connective tissue is reorganised to retain as much function as possible. The connective tissue then gives epithelial cells support to grow on, like a floor to build across. So the epidermis grows over the top at the stage of early fibrous scar.","w":["Basal skin cells replace what is lost, and this is not the reason for the sequence.","The epidermis grows over the top, not down to the base.","Consumption of growth factors by capillaries is not the stated reason.","Macrophages do not form a new epithelium."],"k":"Connective tissue gives a floor for epithelial growth.","rn":[["","Why can epithelial cells proliferate after organisation?","Why can epithelial cells proliferate after organisation?","Connective tissue gives them support to grow on"]]},{"id":45060,"t":"Resolution of Inflammation","s":"In a healing large wound, which step can briefly make the wound <b>bigger</b> rather than smaller?","o":["Phagocytosis","Granulation","Epithelial proliferation","Collagen deposition","Scar contraction"],"e":"In the first step of second intention healing, phagocytosis can make the gap bigger, with more tissue lost. Granulation tissue then lines the base with capillary loops and early myofibroblasts. Collagen fills the gap, the epidermis grows over, and remodelling contracts the scar.","w":["Granulation builds new tissue in the wound base.","Epithelial proliferation covers the wound.","Collagen deposition fills the gap.","Contraction narrows the scar."],"k":"Phagocytosis can enlarge the gap at first.","rn":[["","When do macrophages produce growth factors?","When do macrophages produce growth factors?","After phagocytosis; After inflammation resolution"],["","What removes cell debris during resolution?","What removes cell debris during resolution?","Macrophage phagocytosis"],["","What are the 4 steps of debris phagocytosis?","What are the 4 steps of debris phagocytosis?","Recognition; Binding; Signalling; Phagocytosis"]]},{"id":45061,"t":"Scar Formation","s":"A scar is being remodelled over the weeks after a large wound. Which set of changes is expected?","o":["Early collagen is degraded and replaced by type I, vessels slowly disappear, and the scar contracts","Type I collagen is degraded and replaced by fibrin, vessels multiply, and the scar expands outward","All collagen is removed and replaced by the original epithelium, and the skin returns to normal","Fibroblasts all die at once, new vessels stay permanently leaky, and the scar softens to fluid","Original tissue regrows through the scar from stem cells, and all of the scar's vessels are retained"],"e":"During remodelling, deposited collagen changes to type I collagen and the originally deposited collagen is degraded. Blood vessels slowly disappear, though some remain because the new tissue still needs perfusion. The newly formed scar contracts. The final scar consists of collagen and the remaining fibroblasts.","w":["Fibrin is the earlier weak join, and vessels decrease rather than multiply.","Repair does not return the tissue to its original state.","Remaining fibroblasts are part of the final scar.","A scar is not replaced by original tissue, and vessels largely disappear."],"k":"Remodelling: type I collagen, vessels regress, scar contracts.","rn":[["","What happens to blood vessels in scar remodelling?","What happens to blood vessels in scar remodelling?","Slowly disappear; Some kept for perfusion"],["","Why does a scar not restore the original tissue?","Why does a scar not restore the original tissue?","Collagen is replaced and contracts"]]},{"id":45062,"t":"Scar Formation","s":"A patient asks how strong their fully healed skin scar will be compared with normal skin. What is the most accurate answer?","o":["At most about 80% of normal skin strength","About 100%, once remodelling has finished","About 120%, as scar collagen is stronger","About 10%, because collagen stays weak","It will vary between 150% and 200%"],"e":"Tissue regains functionality, ultimately a maximum of 80% of normal skin strength. In repair it is never 100%. The final scar is type I collagen and remaining fibroblasts, and this does not recreate the original tissue.","w":["In repair the strength is never 100%.","A scar does not exceed the strength of normal skin.","The maximum is far higher than this, at about 80%.","The maximum is 80% of normal skin strength."],"k":"Maximum scar strength: 80% of normal skin.","rn":[["","What is the maximum strength of a scar compared with normal skin?","What is the maximum strength of a scar compared with normal skin?","80%"]]},{"id":45063,"t":"Scar Formation","s":"Which collagen type makes up the final scar in skin?","a":["type i","type 1","type i collagen","type 1 collagen","collagen type i","collagen type 1","collagen i"],"e":"Deposited collagen changes to type I collagen during remodelling, and type I is the type in the skin. The first collagen is not a specific type, because the aim is to cover the wound as fast as possible. The final scar is type I collagen and remaining fibroblasts.","k":"Final skin scar collagen: type I.","rn":[["","What does deposited collagen change to in remodelling?","What does deposited collagen change to in remodelling?","Type I collagen"]]},{"id":45064,"t":"Scar Formation","s":"Why is the first collagen laid down in a wound not of a specific type?","o":["The priority is to cover the wound as fast as possible","Fibroblasts cannot make type I collagen under any conditions","Macrophages have to digest type I before any fibroblast arrives","Specific collagen types only appear in tissues that regenerate","The epidermis must first have grown over the scar's upper surface"],"e":"The first collagen is not a specific type, because the aim is to cover the wound as fast as possible. An open wound is an opportunity for microbes to enter. Later, remodelling degrades this early collagen and replaces it with type I.","w":["Type I collagen appears during remodelling, so fibroblast production is not ruled out.","Macrophages clearing type I collagen first is not the stated reason.","Scars formed by repair do contain type I collagen.","Fast cover is the reason, rather than prior epidermal growth."],"k":"Speed first: early collagen is non-specific, later replaced by type I."},{"id":45065,"t":"Tissue Repair","s":"Why do some blood vessels remain in a mature scar rather than all disappearing?","o":["The new tissue still needs perfusion","The vessels are needed to carry collagen away","The vessels are the source of type I collagen","The vessels supply the neutrophils for the rest of life","The vessels provide the contraction force in the scar"],"e":"During remodelling, blood vessels disappear slowly, and some remain because the new tissue still needs perfusion, meaning blood supply. The scar is left with collagen and the remaining fibroblasts. Contraction relates to the scar tissue and myofibroblasts rather than vessels.","w":["Collagen stays in the scar and is not being carried away.","Fibroblasts deposit collagen.","A lifelong neutrophil supply is not the reason given.","Scar tissue contracts through its connective tissue and not through vessels."],"k":"Some scar vessels remain for perfusion.","rn":[["","Why can a liver with good regenerative capacity still repair instead?","Why can a liver with good regenerative capacity still repair instead?","Tissue architecture destroyed"]]},{"id":45066,"t":"Scar Formation","s":"Which triad defines granulation tissue?","o":["Angiogenesis, proliferating fibroblasts, and remaining inflammatory cells","Mature type I collagen, no vessels, and remaining adult stem cells","Giant cells, epithelioid cells, and central necrosis","Haematoma, callus, and lamellar bone","Keratinocytes, neurons, and terminally differentiated cardiac muscle cells"],"e":"Granulation tissue is characterised by angiogenesis, proliferation of fibroblasts which deposit collagen, and remaining inflammatory cells, usually neutrophils. Its new vessels are leaky and can contribute to oedema and swelling. A mature scar is different, because it is mainly type I collagen and remaining fibroblasts.","w":["This is closer to a mature scar, and new vessels are essential to granulation tissue.","These are features of a granuloma rather than granulation tissue.","These are stages in the healing of a fracture.","These are listed as permanent cells."],"k":"Granulation: new vessels, fibroblasts, remaining inflammatory cells.","rn":[["","What 3 things characterise granulation tissue?","What 3 things characterise granulation tissue?","Angiogenesis; Proliferation of fibroblasts; Remaining inflammatory cells"]]},{"id":45067,"t":"Scar Formation","s":"A bright red, bumpy surface on a healing wound feels sticky. What accounts for this stickiness?","o":["The leaky new vessels let fluid build up on the surface","Mature type I collagen is secreted onto the surface of the skin","Keratinocytes in the wound surface are making a thick glue","Hypochlorite from granules has dissolved all of the local epidermis","Fibrin has been converted back to fibrinogen on the wound surface"],"e":"Granulation tissue looks bright red and bumpy. It feels sticky because the leaky new vessels let fluid build up on the surface. Those same leaky vessels can contribute to oedema and swelling. Most people have seen this tissue after an injury.","w":["Mature collagen is not secreted onto the surface.","Keratinocytes are not described as producing this stickiness.","Hypochlorite is a killing chemical and is not the reason for stickiness.","Fibrinogen is converted to fibrin, rather than the reverse."],"k":"Granulation tissue is sticky because its new vessels leak.","rn":[["","Why does granulation tissue feel sticky?","Why does granulation tissue feel sticky?","Fluid leaks from new vessels"]]},{"id":45068,"t":"Scar Formation","s":"Two wound sections have the same special stain that turns collagen blue. One has fine blue threads and dense red streaks. The other is mostly blue. What is the best interpretation?","o":["The first is granulation tissue, and the second is a more mature collagen-rich scar","The first is a mature scar, and the second is an early granulation tissue with vessels","Both are granulation tissue, as the blue stain marks only the new blood vessels","The first is a keloid, and the second is a normal tissue that has regenerated","Both are normal tissue, as the red streaks indicate fibroblasts producing collagen"],"e":"The stain turns collagen blue. Granulation tissue has very few collagen fibres, seen as fine blue threads, and the dense red streaks are red blood cells. In collagen-rich scar tissue, the field is mostly blue because scar formation has progressed. Blue therefore tracks the maturity of the scar.","w":["This reverses the interpretation of the amount of blue staining.","The blue stain marks collagen rather than vessels.","Sparse collagen does not fit a keloid, which has excess collagen.","The dense red streaks in the section are red blood cells."],"k":"Little blue = granulation; mostly blue = collagen-rich scar.","rn":[["","Image card: Collagen stains blue: which is granulation tissue and which is scar?","Collagen stains blue: which is granulation tissue and which is scar?","A granulation (few collagen fibres); B collagen-rich scar"]]},{"id":45069,"t":"Scar Formation","s":"Name the crust labelled on top of a wound, above the epithelial cells moving in beneath it, in the stage of granulation.","a":["eschar","scab"],"e":"The eschar is the dark red crust on top of the wound. Beneath it, epithelial cells move in, and granulation tissue with new blood vessels fills the wound. Fibroblasts have proliferated and blood vessels have formed after the clear-out by macrophages.","k":"Eschar = crust over a healing wound."},{"id":45070,"t":"Tissue Regeneration vs Repair","s":"In a wound healing by second intention, which cell type lies in the granulation layer and later accounts for a scar's contraction into a narrow wedge?","o":["Myofibroblasts","Hepatocytes","Kupffer cells","Pericytes","Neutrophils"],"e":"Myofibroblasts are fibroblasts that can contract. They appear in a thin layer with collagen in granulation tissue, and they fill the gap with new collagen. In remodelling the connective tissue underneath contracts, leaving a narrow wedge of scar.","w":["Hepatocytes are liver cells.","Kupffer cells are liver macrophages.","Pericytes lie on the outside of vessels.","Neutrophils are remaining inflammatory cells, not contractile cells."],"k":"Myofibroblasts: fibroblasts that can contract.","rn":[["","What do recruited fibroblasts differentiate into in the fibrosis pathway?","What do recruited fibroblasts differentiate into in the fibrosis pathway?","Myofibroblasts"]]},{"id":45071,"t":"Scar Formation","s":"A patient has a raised, pale pink scar that stays along the line of the original wound. How should this be classified?","o":["Hypertrophic scar","Keloid","Primary intention","Chronic ulcer","Granulation tissue"],"e":"A hypertrophic scar is an excessive deposition of collagen, producing a quite visible scar that stays along the line of the wound. A keloid, by contrast, grows beyond the scar boundaries and does not regress. The position relative to the wound line is therefore the discriminator.","w":["A keloid would grow beyond the wound boundaries.","First intention is a healing route rather than a raised abnormal scar.","An ulcer is a loss of tissue and not a raised line.","Granulation tissue is bright red, bumpy and sticky."],"k":"Hypertrophic scar stays along the wound line.","rn":[["","Image card: What is this scar? What causes it?","What is this scar? What causes it?","Hypertrophic scar; Excessive deposition of collagen"],["","What is meant by Hypertrophic Scar?","What is meant by Hypertrophic Scar?","Excessive deposition of collagen"]]},{"id":45072,"t":"Scar Formation","s":"A patient has large, dark, lumpy growths on the shoulder, extending far beyond a small original wound and continuing to enlarge. Which statement fits best?","o":["It is a keloid, which does not regress and may have a genetic predisposition","It is a hypertrophic scar, which is always confined to the line of the wound","It is granulation tissue, which is the sign that repair has just begun","It is a regenerative nodule, which arises when liver architecture is destroyed","It is an ulcer, which is a sign of tissue loss in the epidermis and the dermis"],"e":"A keloid is scar tissue that grows beyond the scar boundaries. It does not regress and continues to grow. It may have a genetic predisposition, although this is not quite clear. Under the microscope, excessive collagen deposition appears in the dermis as thick pink bands.","w":["A hypertrophic scar stays along the line of the wound, which does not fit here.","Granulation tissue is red and sticky and does not spread beyond the wound.","Regenerative nodules are a feature of liver cirrhosis.","An ulcer involves loss of tissue rather than a protruding growth."],"k":"Keloid: beyond boundaries, does not regress, possibly genetic.","rn":[["","What is meant by Keloid?","What is meant by Keloid?","Scar grows beyond its boundaries; Doesn't regress"],["","Image card: What is this scar? How does it behave?","What is this scar? How does it behave?","Keloid; Grows beyond boundaries, doesn't regress"]]},{"id":45073,"t":"Scar Formation","s":"Which histological finding underlies a keloid's protrusion from the skin?","o":["Thick bands of excessive collagen in the dermis","A thick layer of neutrophils in the epidermis","Many multinucleate giant cells around central necrosis","A crater with the epithelium missing on both sides","Hexagonal units separated by regenerative nodules"],"e":"Under the microscope, a keloid shows the epidermis on top with excessive collagen deposition in the dermis, seen as thick pink bands. This makes the scar protrude a lot. It is a disorder of too much scar rather than too little healing.","w":["Neutrophils are not the described cause of the protrusion.","Giant cells around necrosis describe a granuloma.","A crater with missing epithelium describes an ulcer.","Nodules and hexagonal units relate to the liver."],"k":"Keloid = excess dermal collagen.","rn":[["","Image card: Keloid histology: what is the outlined area?","Keloid histology: what is the outlined area?","Excessive collagen deposition; In the dermis"]]},{"id":45074,"t":"Problems in Wound Healing","s":"A patient with vascular disease has a non-healing foot wound. Histology shows a gap in the epithelium with granulation tissue in the dermis. What is the diagnosis?","o":["Skin ulcer","Keloid","Hypertrophic scar","Callus","Regenerative nodule"],"e":"Skin ulcers are chronic wounds that affect the extremities, particularly the feet. They show tissue necrosis and failure to heal. You see ulceration of the epithelium and granulation in the dermis. Restricted blood flow and vascular disease are recognised causes.","w":["A keloid is an excessive scar growing beyond the boundaries.","A hypertrophic scar is excess collagen along the wound.","A callus is the first new bone in a fracture.","A regenerative nodule is a feature of a cirrhotic liver."],"k":"Ulcer: loss of epithelium + granulation in dermis, often on feet.","rn":[["","Where do skin ulcers mainly occur?","Where do skin ulcers mainly occur?","Extremities; Particularly the feet"],["","What are the 4 causes of skin ulcers?","What are the 4 causes of skin ulcers?","Restricted blood flow, vascular disease; Systemic metabolic disorders; Persistent infection; Secondary infection"],["","What 2 features characterise chronic wounds?","What 2 features characterise chronic wounds?","Ulceration of the epithelium; Granulation in the dermis"]]},{"id":45075,"t":"Problems in Wound Healing","s":"What is the general term for loss of tissue, which in the skin means loss of the epidermis and part of the dermis?","a":["ulceration","ulcer"],"e":"Ulceration is loss of tissue. In an epithelium, it means loss of epithelium. In the skin, it means loss of epidermis and part of the dermis. Chronic wounds are therefore called ulcers when there is tissue necrosis and failure to heal.","k":"Ulceration = loss of tissue.","rn":[["","What is lost in skin ulceration?","What is lost in skin ulceration?","Epidermis; Part of dermis"],["","What is meant by Ulceration?","What is meant by Ulceration?","Loss of tissue"]]},{"id":45076,"t":"Scar Formation","s":"Which is <b>not</b> given as a cause of chronic skin ulcers?","o":["Excessive type I collagen deposition beyond the boundary of a scar","Restricted blood flow and vascular disease","Systemic metabolic disorders","Persistent infection","Secondary infection"],"e":"Chronic ulcers are caused by restricted blood flow and vascular disease, systemic metabolic disorders, and persistent or secondary infection. In infection, the inflammatory response has not been very effective and healing occurs before it has finished. Excess collagen beyond the boundary is what happens in a keloid, not an ulcer.","w":["Restricted blood flow is a listed cause.","Systemic metabolic disorders are a listed cause.","Persistent infection is a listed cause.","Secondary infection is a listed cause."],"k":"Ulcer causes: poor flow/vascular disease, metabolic disorders, infection.","rn":[["","Image card: What is this scar? What causes it?","What is this scar? What causes it?","Hypertrophic scar; Excessive deposition of collagen"],["","What is meant by Hypertrophic Scar?","What is meant by Hypertrophic Scar?","Excessive deposition of collagen"]]},{"id":45077,"t":"Problems in Wound Healing","s":"A patient has a long, narrow wound in dark, discoloured skin near the ankle. Which ulcer type does this resemble most?","o":["Venous leg ulcer","Arterial ulcer","Diabetic ulcer","Pressure sore","Keloid scar of the ankle"],"e":"A venous leg ulcer is a long, narrow wound in dark, discoloured skin near the ankle. An arterial ulcer is a large red wound with sharp edges on the lower leg. A diabetic ulcer is on the sole of the foot with a dark necrotic centre. A pressure sore is a deep crater with yellow material inside.","w":["An arterial ulcer is a large red wound with sharp edges on the lower leg.","A diabetic ulcer lies on the sole with a dark necrotic centre.","A pressure sore is a deep crater with yellow material.","A keloid is a protruding growth rather than a wound."],"k":"Venous ulcer: long, narrow, discoloured skin near the ankle.","rn":[["","Image card: Name the 4 skin ulcers, A to D","Name the 4 skin ulcers, A to D","A venous leg ulcer; B arterial ulcer; C diabetic ulcer; D pressure sore"]]},{"id":45078,"t":"Problems in Wound Healing","s":"A diabetic patient has an ulcer on the sole of the foot. Which appearance is most consistent with this ulcer type?","o":["A dark necrotic centre on the sole","A long narrow wound near the ankle","A large sharp-edged red wound on the calf","A deep crater with yellow material over a bony area","A raised pale line along an old cut"],"e":"The diabetic ulcer is on the sole of the foot with a dark necrotic centre. Diabetes mellitus also impairs the function of leucocytes, which is a systemic factor in poor healing. Ulcers in general combine tissue necrosis with failure to heal.","w":["This describes a venous leg ulcer.","This describes an arterial ulcer.","This describes a pressure sore.","This describes a hypertrophic scar."],"k":"Diabetic ulcer: sole of foot, dark necrotic centre."},{"id":45079,"t":"Scar Formation","s":"Under the microscope, what would you expect at the base of a chronic skin ulcer?","o":["Dense inflammatory cells with small vessels in the tissue beneath","Neat rows of intact epithelium with no inflammatory cells","Only mature type I collagen with no vessels at all","Regenerative nodules separated by broad bands of fibrous tissue","Lamellar bone replacing a woven bone callus"],"e":"The base of an ulcer shows chronic inflammation and granulation: dense dark inflammatory cells near the surface and small vessels in paler tissue beneath. The edges of the crater show the epithelium stopping on each side, leaving a gap where tissue is lost.","w":["The epithelium is missing at the ulcer rather than intact.","The ulcer base has inflammatory cells and small vessels.","Regenerative nodules are a feature of liver cirrhosis.","Lamellar bone is a feature of fracture healing."],"k":"Ulcer base: chronic inflammation and granulation.","rn":[["","Which inflammatory cells usually remain in granulation tissue?","Which inflammatory cells usually remain in granulation tissue?","Neutrophils"],["","What 3 things characterise granulation tissue?","What 3 things characterise granulation tissue?","Angiogenesis; Proliferation of fibroblasts; Remaining inflammatory cells"]]},{"id":45080,"t":"Problems in Wound Healing","s":"Which option consists only of <b>local</b> factors impairing wound healing?","o":["Foreign body, haematoma and denervation","Foreign body, diabetes mellitus and obesity","Age, malnutrition and vitamin C deficiency","Steroids, Marfan's and haematoma","Trace mineral deficiency, necrotic tissue and age"],"e":"Local factors act at the wound and include cause and site, persistent injury, local infection, low white blood cell counts, foreign body, haematoma, denervation, poor perfusion, mechanical or iatrogenic stress, and necrotic tissue. Systemic factors include age, drugs, soft tissue genetic disorders, diabetes, malnutrition, obesity, vitamin C deficiency and trace mineral deficiencies.","w":["Diabetes mellitus and obesity are systemic factors.","All three of these are systemic factors.","Steroids and Marfan's are systemic factors.","Trace mineral deficiency and age are systemic factors."],"k":"Local: foreign body, haematoma, denervation, perfusion, necrotic tissue, etc.","rn":[["","Which 3 wound contents impair healing locally?","Which 3 wound contents impair healing locally?","Foreign body; Haematoma; Necrotic tissue"]]},{"id":45081,"t":"Problems in Wound Healing","s":"Which option consists only of <b>systemic</b> factors impairing wound healing?","o":["Age, cell cycle inhibitors and Ehlers-Danlos","Age, foreign body and persistent pressure","Denervation, poor perfusion and haematoma","Local infection, necrotic tissue and obesity","Mechanical stress, malnutrition and diabetes"],"e":"Age, drugs such as cell cycle inhibitors, and soft tissue genetic disorders such as Ehlers-Danlos and Marfan's are systemic factors. Foreign bodies, pressure, denervation, poor perfusion, haematoma, local infection, necrotic tissue and mechanical stress all act at the wound.","w":["A foreign body and persistent pressure are local factors.","All three of these are local factors.","Local infection and necrotic tissue are local factors.","Mechanical stress is a local factor."],"k":"Systemic: age, drugs, genetic disorders, diabetes, nutrition, obesity.","rn":[["","Which 2 soft tissue genetic disorders impair healing?","Which 2 soft tissue genetic disorders impair healing?","Ehlers-Danlos; Marfan's"],["","Which 3 drug groups impair wound healing?","Which 3 drug groups impair wound healing?","Steroids; Antibiotics; Cell cycle inhibitors"]]},{"id":45082,"t":"Problems in Wound Healing","s":"A bed-bound patient has a wound over the sacrum that keeps breaking down despite good nutrition. Which local factor is most relevant?","o":["Persistent injury from pressure","Denervation of the local skin","Vitamin C deficiency","Old age alone","A retained foreign body"],"e":"Persistent injury, as in pressure wounds, is a local factor, and bed-bound patients are the example because the injury keeps going. Healing cannot proceed if the injury continues. Vitamin C deficiency and age are systemic factors rather than local ones.","w":["Nothing here indicates loss of the nerve supply.","Vitamin C deficiency is systemic, and nutrition here is good.","Age is systemic and does not explain repeated breakdown from pressure.","Nothing here points to a retained object."],"k":"Pressure wounds: persistent injury in bed-bound patients.","rn":[["","Which 3 injury factors impair healing locally?","Which 3 injury factors impair healing locally?","Cause and site of injury; Persistent injury (pressure wounds); Mechanical or iatrogenic stress"]]},{"id":45083,"t":"Problems in Wound Healing","s":"A patient's wound over an area with poor perfusion heals slowly. Which explanation is correct?","o":["Poorer inflammatory response, and less delivery of growth factors, cells and resources","More neutrophil delivery, leading to rapid healing but a keloid across the whole of the wound in all cases","Excess oxygen delivery, leading to more reactive species outside phagocytes","Increased stem cell number, which then blocks the fibroblasts from entering the wound","Increased local growth factors, which permanently stop all further cell proliferation"],"e":"Poor blood supply or perfusion gives a poorer inflammatory response and less delivery of growth factors, cells and resources. Each stage of healing depends on these. This is why restricted blood flow and vascular disease also appear among the causes of chronic ulcers.","w":["Poor perfusion reduces the delivery of neutrophils.","Poor perfusion does not deliver excess oxygen.","Poor perfusion does not raise stem cell numbers.","Poor perfusion delivers fewer growth factors rather than more."],"k":"Poor perfusion: weak inflammation and less delivery of resources.","rn":[["","Why does poor perfusion impair healing?","Why does poor perfusion impair healing?","Weak inflammatory response; Growth factors, cells not delivered"]]},{"id":45084,"t":"Problems in Wound Healing","s":"A patient with a low white blood cell count has a wound that keeps getting worse. What is the most direct reason?","o":["A weaker inflammatory response, with potentially more spread of infection","Greater regenerative capacity, leading to over-rapid wound closure","More collagen deposition, leading to a keloid over all of the wound","Fewer adult stem cells, leading to absent regeneration from birth onward in every tissue of the body","Greater vasodilation, leading to permanent oedema with no infection"],"e":"Low white blood cell counts cause a weaker inflammatory response and potentially more spread of infection. Clearing pathogens and debris is required for resolution. It is listed among local factors in wound healing.","w":["A low count does not increase regenerative capacity.","A low count is not linked to excessive collagen deposition here.","The described issue is the white cell response, not a lifelong lack of stem cells.","Greater vasodilation is not the described consequence."],"k":"Low WBC: weak response, spread of infection.","rn":[["","Why do low white blood cell counts impair wound healing?","Why do low white blood cell counts impair wound healing?","Weaker inflammatory response; More spread of infection"]]},{"id":45085,"t":"Problems in Wound Healing","s":"What is the term for the loss of nerve supply to a wound area, which is listed as a local factor impairing healing?","a":["denervation"],"e":"Denervation is the loss of nerve supply to the area, and it is listed among the local factors impairing wound healing. Others include foreign bodies, haematoma, poor perfusion, mechanical or iatrogenic stress and necrotic tissue.","k":"Denervation = loss of nerve supply; a local factor.","rn":[["","Which 4 supply and defence factors impair healing locally?","Which 4 supply and defence factors impair healing locally?","Local infection; Low white blood cell counts; Poor blood supply or perfusion; Denervation"]]},{"id":45086,"t":"Problems in Wound Healing","s":"Which term describes stress on a wound caused by medical treatment, listed as a local factor impairing healing?","a":["iatrogenic","iatrogenic stress"],"e":"Mechanical or iatrogenic stress means physical strain on the wound or stress caused by medical treatment. It is a local factor impairing healing. It sits alongside other local factors acting at the wound such as foreign body and necrotic tissue.","k":"Iatrogenic = caused by medical treatment.","rn":[["","Which 3 injury factors impair healing locally?","Which 3 injury factors impair healing locally?","Cause and site of injury; Persistent injury (pressure wounds); Mechanical or iatrogenic stress"]]},{"id":45087,"t":"Problems in Wound Healing","s":"A burn on the foot and a small cut on the lip have very different healing courses. Which local factor explains this?","o":["Cause and site of injury","Denervation alone","Soft tissue genetic disorder","Vitamin C deficiency","Cell cycle inhibitor drugs"],"e":"Cause and site of injury, such as burns versus cuts and lip versus foot, is a local factor, because some areas of the body are more complicated to heal. The other options either need specific evidence or are systemic rather than local.","w":["Nothing here indicates loss of nerve supply.","A genetic disorder is systemic and would not explain two different sites.","Vitamin C deficiency is systemic and would affect both wounds.","These drugs are systemic and would affect both wounds."],"k":"Cause and site of injury affect healing.","rn":[["","Which 3 injury factors impair healing locally?","Which 3 injury factors impair healing locally?","Cause and site of injury; Persistent injury (pressure wounds); Mechanical or iatrogenic stress"]]},{"id":45088,"t":"Problems in Wound Healing","s":"A patient on long-term steroids has a slow-healing wound. Why might this drug class impair healing?","o":["Steroids are anti-inflammatory, and healing depends on an effective inflammatory phase","Steroids are cell-killing radiation agents, destroying all fibroblasts in the wound and all of the epithelium","Steroids are antibiotics, and therefore they remove all of the wound's useful bacteria","Steroids are made of collagen, which fills the wound and blocks any new matrix","Steroids act locally only, as a foreign body that remains within the wound bed"],"e":"Drugs are a systemic factor impairing healing. These include steroids, which are anti-inflammatory, antibiotics, and cell cycle inhibitors such as radiation and cytotoxic therapies. An effective inflammatory phase is the first step of healing, so damping it can frustrate recovery.","w":["Radiation and cytotoxic therapies are the cell cycle inhibitors, not steroids.","Steroids are listed separately from antibiotics.","Steroids are not made of collagen.","Steroids are systemic, not a foreign body."],"k":"Steroids (anti-inflammatory) impair healing systemically.","rn":[["","What type of drug are steroids?","What type of drug are steroids?","Anti-inflammatory"]]},{"id":45089,"t":"Problems in Wound Healing","s":"A patient receiving cytotoxic therapy heals poorly. Which explanation best links the therapy to the process of healing?","o":["Cell cycle inhibition interferes with the proliferation that healing relies on","Cytotoxic therapy increases adult stem cell numbers so much that they crowd the wound","Cytotoxic therapy causes keloids that grow beyond the wound and stop re-epithelialisation","Cytotoxic therapy turns hepatocytes into permanent cells throughout the body","Cytotoxic therapy releases phosphatidylserine onto healthy skin, so all skin is eaten"],"e":"Cell cycle inhibitors, such as radiation and cytotoxic therapies, are listed among the drug-related systemic factors impairing healing. Regeneration and repair both rely on proliferation of stem cells, fibroblasts, endothelial cells and epithelial cells. Inhibiting cell division therefore interferes with this.","w":["These therapies do not increase stem cell numbers.","Keloid formation is not the described effect.","Conversion of hepatocytes to permanent cells is not described.","This mechanism is not described."],"k":"Cell cycle inhibitors impede proliferation-dependent healing.","rn":[["","Which 2 therapies act as cell cycle inhibitors?","Which 2 therapies act as cell cycle inhibitors?","Radiation; Cytotoxic therapies"],["","Which 3 drug groups impair wound healing?","Which 3 drug groups impair wound healing?","Steroids; Antibiotics; Cell cycle inhibitors"]]},{"id":45090,"t":"Problems in Wound Healing","s":"A patient with diabetes mellitus heals poorly. Which specific mechanism is given?","o":["Impaired function of leucocytes","Absence of fibrinogen in the blood","Loss of all phosphatidylserine","Excess vitamin C inside the wound","Failure of G0 to G1 priming only"],"e":"Diabetes mellitus impairs the function of leucocytes, which are white blood cells. This weakens the inflammatory phase and so the clearing of pathogens and debris. Diabetes is a systemic factor, and diabetic ulcers are one type of chronic skin ulcer.","w":["Absent fibrinogen is not the described mechanism.","Loss of phosphatidylserine is not the described mechanism.","Vitamin C deficiency, rather than excess, is the factor listed.","Priming of hepatocytes is specific to liver regeneration."],"k":"Diabetes impairs leucocyte function.","rn":[["","How does diabetes mellitus impair wound healing?","How does diabetes mellitus impair wound healing?","Impairs leucocyte function"]]},{"id":45091,"t":"Problems in Wound Healing","s":"Which pair of soft tissue genetic disorders is listed as systemic factors impairing wound healing?","o":["Ehlers-Danlos and Marfan's","Crohn disease and cirrhosis","Keloid and hypertrophic scar","Tuberculosis and leprosy","Venous and arterial ulcers"],"e":"Soft tissue genetic disorders such as Ehlers-Danlos and Marfan's are systemic factors impairing wound healing. They are listed with age, drugs, diabetes, malnutrition, obesity, vitamin C deficiency and trace mineral deficiencies.","w":["These are not the soft tissue genetic disorders named.","These are types of excessive scar.","These are infectious conditions rather than soft tissue genetic disorders.","These are types of chronic ulcer."],"k":"Genetic factors: Ehlers-Danlos, Marfan's.","rn":[["","Which 2 soft tissue genetic disorders impair healing?","Which 2 soft tissue genetic disorders impair healing?","Ehlers-Danlos; Marfan's"]]},{"id":45092,"t":"Problems in Wound Healing","s":"A patient with poor diet has a wound that heals poorly, with low vitamin C and trace minerals. What is the shared reason these impair healing?","o":["There are no resources to sustain healing","They specifically increase wound contraction","They directly cause phosphatidylserine loss","They trigger keloids that do not regress","They convert fibroblasts to neutrophils"],"e":"Malnutrition or poor nutrition, vitamin C deficiency and trace mineral deficiencies are systemic factors. The common reason is that there are no resources to sustain healing. Healing is demanding work, involving proliferation of cells and deposition of matrix.","w":["Greater wound contraction is not the stated effect.","Phosphatidylserine loss is not the stated effect.","Keloid formation is not the stated effect.","Fibroblasts do not convert into neutrophils."],"k":"Poor nutrition, low vitamin C or trace minerals: no resources for healing."},{"id":45093,"t":"Problems in Wound Healing","s":"A patient has progressive breathlessness, and lung tissue shows extensive scarring from a long fibrotic process. Which general principle does this illustrate?","o":["Excessive fibrosis can lead to loss of function of a high-functioning organ","Scarring is always beneficial, because it makes the organ stronger than before","Fibrosis is confined to the skin and occurs in no other organ of the body","Scar tissue regenerates into the original tissue after a few weeks of healing","Permanent cells proliferate in response to fibrosis and so replace lost function"],"e":"Excessive tissue fibrosis can lead to fibrotic disorders, such as liver cirrhosis and lung fibrosis, leading to loss of organ function. A scarred part of a high-functioning organ can no longer do its normal job. So repair is useful, but excess repair is itself a problem.","w":["Scarring in high-functioning organs impairs function.","Liver cirrhosis and lung fibrosis show fibrosis occurs in organs.","Repair does not return the tissue to its original state.","Permanent cells cannot proliferate."],"k":"Excess fibrosis → loss of organ function (cirrhosis, lung fibrosis).","rn":[["","What can excessive tissue fibrosis lead to?","What can excessive tissue fibrosis lead to?","Fibrotic disorders; Loss of organ function"]]},{"id":45094,"t":"Problems in Wound Healing","s":"A chronic gastrointestinal ulcer progresses from the luminal side outward. In which order would it damage the layers of the wall?","o":["Mucosal epithelium, submucosal collagen, then muscular layer","Muscular layer, submucosal collagen, then mucosal epithelium","Submucosal collagen, mucosal epithelium, then muscular layer","Mucosal epithelium, muscular layer, then submucosal collagen","Muscular layer, mucosal epithelium, then submucosal collagen"],"e":"The mucosal layer is made of epithelial cells, and the submucosal layer consists of collagen fibres. A chronic ulcer progressively damages the mucosal epithelium and, in severe cases, the injury can reach the muscular layer. An ulcer starts on the mucosal side and works towards the muscle.","w":["This reverses the direction of progression.","The ulcer starts in the mucosa, on the luminal side.","The submucosa lies between the mucosa and the muscle.","The ulcer begins on the mucosal side rather than in muscle."],"k":"GI ulcer: mucosa → submucosa → muscle.","rn":[["","What does the submucosal layer consist of?","What does the submucosal layer consist of?","Collagen fibres"],["","What layer can a chronic ulcer eventually reach?","What layer can a chronic ulcer eventually reach?","Muscular layer"],["","What is the mucosal layer made of?","What is the mucosal layer made of?","Epithelial cells"]]},{"id":45095,"t":"Problems in Wound Healing","s":"Which layer of the gastrointestinal wall consists of collagen fibres?","a":["submucosa","submucosal layer","submucosal","the submucosa"],"e":"The mucosal layer is made of epithelial cells and the submucosal layer consists of collagen fibres. Outside this is the smooth muscle layer. A chronic ulcer damages the mucosal epithelium progressively and in severe cases can reach the muscular layer.","k":"Submucosa = collagen fibres."},{"id":45096,"t":"Summary of Wound Healing","s":"What is the correct order of the four stages of wound healing in skin?","o":["Haemostasis, inflammation, proliferation, remodelling","Inflammation, haemostasis, remodelling, proliferation","Proliferation, haemostasis, inflammation, remodelling","Haemostasis, proliferation, inflammation, remodelling","Remodelling, inflammation, haemostasis, proliferation"],"e":"Haemostasis forms a fibrin-platelet clot to stop bleeding and prevent infection. Inflammation recruits immune cells. Proliferation brings keratinocytes, fibroblasts and endothelial cells to the wound site, and remodelling removes inflammatory cells and necrotic material and remodels matrix. Damage repair is then reached at about 14 days.","w":["The clot forms before inflammation, and proliferation comes before remodelling.","Haemostasis comes first.","Inflammation comes before proliferation.","Remodelling is the last stage."],"k":"Haemostasis → inflammation → proliferation → remodelling.","rn":[["","What are the 4 phases of wound healing in skin?","What are the 4 phases of wound healing in skin?","Haemostasis; Inflammation; Proliferation; Remodelling"]]},{"id":45097,"t":"Summary of Wound Healing","s":"Which timing statement about skin wound healing is correct?","o":["Proliferation is within 12 hours, remodelling from 3 to 4 days, and damage repair by 14 days","Proliferation is within 14 days, remodelling within 12 hours, and damage repair by 4 days","Proliferation starts at 14 days, remodelling at 60 days, and damage repair at one year","Haemostasis takes 14 days, proliferation takes 60 days, and remodelling takes one hour","Remodelling is within 12 hours, haemostasis at 3 to 4 days, and damage repair by 14 days"],"e":"In the skin summary, proliferation is within 12 hours after injury. Remodelling is 3 to 4 days after wounding. Damage repair is 14 days after wounding. Haemostasis comes first of all, with damage recognition.","w":["This mixes up the timings of proliferation and remodelling.","Proliferation begins within 12 hours rather than at 14 days.","Haemostasis is the first stage and these timings do not match.","Remodelling comes at 3 to 4 days rather than within 12 hours."],"k":"12 hours: proliferation; 3–4 days: remodelling; 14 days: damage repair.","rn":[["","When is damage repair reached?","When is damage repair reached?","14 days after wounding"],["","When does cell migration to the wound begin?","When does cell migration to the wound begin?","Within 12 hours after injury"]]},{"id":45098,"t":"Summary of Wound Healing","s":"Which event belongs to the proliferation stage in skin?","o":["Re-epithelialisation, angiogenesis and dermal repair","Formation of the fibrin-platelet clot to stop bleeding","Recruitment of neutrophils by chemokines and cytokines","Removal of necrotic material and matrix remodelling","Regeneration of hair follicles in the large wound only"],"e":"The proliferation stage involves proliferation and migration of keratinocytes, fibroblasts and endothelial cells to the wound site. It includes epidermal re-epithelialisation, angiogenesis and dermal repair, leading to granulation tissue. Clot formation, immune recruitment and matrix remodelling belong to other stages.","w":["The clot is formed during haemostasis.","Immune cell recruitment defines inflammation.","Removal of necrotic material and matrix remodelling belong to the remodelling stage.","Follicle regeneration in large wounds is in the remodelling stage."],"k":"Proliferation: re-epithelialisation, angiogenesis, dermal repair.","rn":[["","What 3 processes occur in the proliferation phase?","What 3 processes occur in the proliferation phase?","Epidermal re-epithelialization; Angiogenesis; Dermal repair"]]},{"id":45099,"t":"Summary of Wound Healing","s":"What difference is described between large and small skin wounds in the remodelling stage?","o":["Large wounds can regenerate hair follicles; small wounds form dermal scar without them","Small wounds can regenerate hair follicles; large wounds form dermal scar without them","Large wounds have no remodelling stage at all; small wounds regenerate follicles and also form dermal scar","Both large and small wounds regenerate all follicles and never form dermal scar","Neither large nor small wounds involve the extracellular matrix during remodelling"],"e":"In the remodelling stage, large wounds show regeneration of hair follicles, called wound-induced hair follicle neogenesis. In small wounds, follicles are not regenerated and dermal scar tissue forms. Both involve removal of inflammatory cells and necrotic material and remodelling of the extracellular matrix.","w":["This reverses the stated difference.","Large wounds do undergo a remodelling stage.","Small wounds do not regenerate follicles.","Remodelling of the extracellular matrix is central to this stage."],"k":"Large wounds: follicle neogenesis; small: dermal scar.","rn":[["","Hair follicles: large vs small wounds","Hair follicles: large vs small wounds","Large: regenerated; Small: not regenerated, dermal scar"]]},{"id":45100,"t":"Summary of Wound Healing","s":"From where does epidermal re-epithelialisation proceed in a healing skin wound?","o":["A proliferative, migrating leading edge at a distance from the wound edge","The centre of the fibrin clot, outward to all sides of the wound equally","The deep muscle fibres, upward through the wound without any matrix support","Macrophages at the wound base, which later differentiate into keratinocytes","Red blood cells in the scab, which later differentiate into keratinocytes"],"e":"Epidermal re-epithelialisation comes from a proliferative, migrating leading edge at a distance from the wound edge. This is part of the proliferation stage. Keratinocytes, fibroblasts and endothelial cells proliferate and migrate into the wound site.","w":["It does not begin in the centre of the clot.","Muscle fibres are not the source of the new epithelium.","Macrophages do not become keratinocytes.","Red blood cells do not become keratinocytes."],"k":"Re-epithelialisation: migrating edge at a distance from the wound.","rn":[["","Where does epidermal re-epithelialization start?","Where does epidermal re-epithelialization start?","Proliferative, migrating leading edge; Located away from the wound edge"]]},{"id":45101,"t":"Summary of Wound Healing","s":"Name the first stage of skin wound healing, which forms a fibrin-platelet clot to stop bleeding and prevent infection.","a":["haemostasis","hemostasis"],"e":"Haemostasis is the first stage, forming a fibrin-platelet clot that stops bleeding and prevents infection. It is linked to damage recognition. Inflammation, proliferation and remodelling follow. The coagulation cascade and platelets are responsible for clotting after injury.","k":"Haemostasis: fibrin-platelet clot, first stage.","rn":[["","What happens in haemostasis?","What happens in haemostasis?","Blood clot (fibrin-platelet) forms; Stops bleeding, prevents infection"]]},{"id":45102,"t":"Tissue Repair","s":"A patient has an injury affecting tissues of different regenerative capacity. Which ranking from most to least capacity is best supported?","o":["Skin epithelium with stem cells, then stable hepatocytes, then permanent neurons","Permanent neurons, then stable hepatocytes, then skin epithelium with stem cells","Stable hepatocytes, then permanent neurons, then skin epithelium with stem cells","Permanent neurons, then skin epithelium with stem cells, then stable hepatocytes","All three of these tissues have an equal regenerative capacity after any injury, as each has adult stem cells"],"e":"Some tissues have more regenerative capacity than others, namely labile compared with stable or permanent cells, and adult stem cells are the key. Skin has a reservoir of adult stem cells. Hepatocytes are stable cells with good capacity when architecture is intact. Neurons are permanent cells with no regenerative capacity.","w":["Neurons have no regenerative capacity, so they cannot rank first.","Neurons cannot rank above skin epithelium.","Neurons have no regenerative capacity.","Tissues do differ in their regenerative capacity."],"k":"Capacity: labile > stable > permanent; adult stem cells are key.","rn":[["","What are the 3 groups of cells by regenerative capacity?","What are the 3 groups of cells by regenerative capacity?","Labile; Stable; Permanent"],["","Which 3 tissues have quiescent adult stem cells?","Which 3 tissues have quiescent adult stem cells?","Intestinal epithelium; Skin; Bone marrow"]]},{"id":45103,"t":"Macrophages and Growth Factors","s":"Which statement best summarises why macrophages are essential to a return to health after injury?","o":["They provide a clean environment, then release growth factors that stimulate repair","They directly become new epithelium, connective tissue and blood vessels in the wound","They block every signal for fibroblasts, ensuring that no collagen is made in the wound","They only act during the initial bleeding, and they leave before inflammation begins","They replace all adult stem cells, so that stem cells are never needed for regeneration"],"e":"Macrophages are the essential cells in injury recovery, providing a clean environment for healing by removing debris. They also produce growth factors that stimulate tissue repair following their phagocytic function and resolution of inflammation. In this way they link the clearing phase to the rebuilding phase.","w":["They do not become new epithelium or vessels.","Macrophage growth factors stimulate tissue repair rather than blocking it.","Macrophages act through inflammation and into repair.","Regeneration relies on adult stem cells."],"k":"Macrophages clean first, then signal repair.","rn":[["","What do macrophage growth factors stimulate?","What do macrophage growth factors stimulate?","Tissue repair"]]},{"id":45104,"t":"Tissue Regeneration vs Repair","s":"A surgeon closes a thin scalpel incision. A week later, a trauma patient has a large crater with tissue lost. Which option correctly contrasts the two wounds?","o":["The first joins by fibrin and collagen; the second fills by granulation tissue and scars","The first fills by granulation tissue and scars; the second joins by fibrin and collagen","Both heal by first intention, as they share fibroblast and collagen involvement and begin with a fibrin join","Both heal by second intention, as they both involve a break in the epidermis","The first needs a callus; the second needs a haematoma and lamellar replacement"],"e":"First intention occurs in minor aligned wounds, with a weak fibrin join followed by a strong collagen join. Second intention occurs in large wounds where tissue is lost and sides cannot align, and it proceeds through granulation tissue to scar. The size of the gap and alignment of the sides decide the route.","w":["This reverses the two routes.","Tissue loss with sides unable to align is not first intention.","A thin aligned cut is not second intention.","Callus and lamellar replacement are stages in fracture healing."],"k":"First: aligned, fibrin→collagen. Second: tissue loss, granulation→scar.","rn":[["","What are the 3 steps of first intention healing?","What are the 3 steps of first intention healing?","Exudation of fibrinogen; Weak fibrin join; Strong collagen join, epidermal regrowth"],["","Image card: Collagen stains blue: which is granulation tissue and which is scar?","Collagen stains blue: which is granulation tissue and which is scar?","A granulation (few collagen fibres); B collagen-rich scar"]]},{"id":45105,"t":"Summary of Wound Healing","s":"An ulcer develops at a wound where infection persisted and healing began before the response had finished. Why does this cause a chronic wound?","o":["Inflammation has not been effective, so healing is proceeding with its work unfinished","Inflammation has ended too completely, so no fibroblast can be recruited to the wound","Infection provides resources to sustain healing, so the wound heals rapidly without scarring","Infection turns stable cells into permanent cells across the wound, preventing proliferation","Infection removes all phosphatidylserine, which is the only requirement for wound closure"],"e":"Persistent or secondary infection is a cause of chronic ulcers. The inflammatory response has not been very effective, and healing occurs before it has finished. This conflicts with the requirement for quick destruction of pathogens and quick removal of debris before healing.","w":["The inflammation has been ineffective rather than too complete.","Infection impairs healing and does not sustain it.","Conversion to permanent cells is not described.","Phosphatidylserine marks dying cells and is not a condition of wound closure."],"k":"Persistent infection: healing starts before inflammation has finished.","rn":[["","What are the 4 phases of wound healing in skin?","What are the 4 phases of wound healing in skin?","Haemostasis; Inflammation; Proliferation; Remodelling"],["","Wound healing: what signals in the inflammation phase?","Wound healing: what signals in the inflammation phase?","Proinflammatory chemokines; Cytokines"],["","What 2 things collectively describe wound healing?","What 2 things collectively describe wound healing?","Inflammation resolution; Tissue regeneration or repair"]]},{"id":45106,"t":"Problems in Wound Healing","s":"A wound contains a retained object and dead tissue that have not been removed. Which conclusion is most appropriate?","o":["Both are local factors that impair healing and work against quick debris removal","Both are systemic factors, which act on the whole body and not on the wound","Both accelerate regeneration, because they give macrophages more material to eat","Both are required for scar remodelling, which cannot occur without them being present","Neither is relevant to healing, as only vitamin C determines the healing rate"],"e":"A foreign body and necrotic tissue are both listed as local factors impairing healing. Foreign bodies should not be in the wound, and the presence of necrotic tissue impairs healing. Quick removal of debris is also a condition for resolution of inflammation.","w":["Both act at the wound, so they are local.","Their presence impairs healing and does not accelerate it.","Remodelling does not require them.","Both are listed among the factors affecting healing."],"k":"Foreign body and necrotic tissue: local impediments.","rn":[["","Which 4 supply and defence factors impair healing locally?","Which 4 supply and defence factors impair healing locally?","Local infection; Low white blood cell counts; Poor blood supply or perfusion; Denervation"],["","Which 3 wound contents impair healing locally?","Which 3 wound contents impair healing locally?","Foreign body; Haematoma; Necrotic tissue"],["","What are the 5 conditions for resolution to proceed?","What are the 5 conditions for resolution to proceed?","Minimal cell death, tissue damage; Regenerative capacity of tissue; Quick destruction of pathogens; Quick removal of debris, fluids; End of vasodilation"]]},{"id":45107,"t":"Tissue Repair","s":"A haematoma helps a fracture heal, yet a haematoma in a skin wound is listed as a factor that impairs healing. How can both be true?","o":["In bone it gives the initial structure for repair; in a wound it is a collection that impairs healing","In bone it is a foreign body that delays repair; in a wound it gives the first structure of the new fibrous scar","In bone it is made of collagen; in a wound it is made of keratinocytes from the basal layer","In bone it forms many months later; in a wound it can form only in patients with diabetes","In bone it is a type of callus; in a wound it is a type of keloid growing past the boundaries"],"e":"After a fracture, a haematoma of coagulated blood forms immediately and provides the initial structure for repair. In a skin wound, a collection of clotted blood is listed as a local factor impairing healing. The same material therefore has a different role depending on the context.","w":["This reverses the two roles.","A haematoma is coagulated blood in both settings.","In bone it forms immediately, and it is not limited to diabetes in a wound.","Callus forms later in bone, and a keloid is an excessive scar."],"k":"Haematoma: helpful scaffold in bone; impediment in a skin wound.","rn":[["","What provides initial structure for bone repair?","What provides initial structure for bone repair?","Haematoma (coagulated blood)"]]},{"id":45108,"t":"Tissue Regeneration vs Repair","s":"Which statement correctly describes why collagen replacement and contraction distinguish scar formation from regeneration?","o":["The tissue does not return to its initial state after these changes","The tissue returns to its initial state because contraction restores it","The tissue loses all connective tissue during contraction, leaving only epithelium","The tissue gains stem cells from collagen, which then regenerate the original structure","The tissue reaches 100% of its normal strength, which then defines the scar"],"e":"Scar formation is not the same as regeneration, because collagen is replaced and contracts, so the tissue does not return to its initial state. A surface may close but dip slightly because of contraction. Regeneration, by contrast, fully regains complete functionality and returns to the normal state.","w":["Contraction does not restore the initial state.","A scar is made of connective tissue rather than losing it.","Collagen does not produce stem cells.","Scar strength reaches at most 80%."],"k":"Collagen replacement and contraction: not the initial state."},{"id":45109,"t":"Resolution of Inflammation","s":"Which statement about what phagocytosis removes in wound healing is correct?","o":["It eliminates necrotic cells as well as apoptotic cells","It removes apoptotic cells but never necrotic cells","It removes necrotic cells but never apoptotic cells","It removes only fluids from the extracellular space","It removes only healthy cells with intact membranes"],"e":"Tissue-resident macrophages recognise apoptotic cells or bodies and eliminate them through phagocytosis. Phagocytosis is used to eliminate necrotic cells as well. Ingesting fluids is a separate activity called pinocytosis. Cells displaying a \"don't eat me\" signal are spared.","w":["Necrotic cells are also eliminated by phagocytosis.","Apoptotic cells are a major target of phagocytosis.","Ingesting fluids is pinocytosis.","Healthy cells are not the target."],"k":"Phagocytosis removes apoptotic and necrotic cells.","rn":[["","What do tissue resident macrophages recognise and eliminate?","What do tissue resident macrophages recognise and eliminate?","Apoptotic cells; Apoptotic bodies"],["","Besides pathogens, what else does phagocytosis eliminate?","Besides pathogens, what else does phagocytosis eliminate?","Necrotic cells"]]},{"id":45110,"t":"Resolution of Inflammation","s":"Macrophages can recognise dying cells by several kinds of receptor. Besides phosphatidylserine receptors, what is the other class bound to C1q, C3b and C4?","o":["Complement receptors such as CR1, CR3 and CR4","Epidermal growth factor receptors such as MET","G-protein receptors such as histamine H1 and H2","Don't eat me receptors such as SIRPα and CD47","Basement membrane receptors such as angiopoietin"],"e":"Labelled complement receptors CR1, CR3 and/or CR4 bind the complement factors C1q, C3b and/or C4. This adds to the many phosphatidylserine receptors. The point is not the names but that macrophages have many receptors for recognising a dying cell.","w":["MET is the receptor for hepatocyte growth factor.","Histamine receptors are not listed here.","CD47 and SIRPα are \"don't eat me\" signalling.","Angiopoietin is linked with pericyte detachment."],"k":"Complement receptors bind C1q, C3b, C4 on dying cells."},{"id":45111,"t":"Macrophages and Growth Factors","s":"A wound needs fibroblasts mobilised to build connective tissue. Which growth factor is most directly linked with this?","a":["fibroblast growth factor","fgf"],"e":"Fibroblast growth factor (FGF) is linked with wound healing mobilising fibroblasts, which differentiate and proliferate to build connective tissue. Other named growth factors are transforming growth factor and platelet-derived growth factor. Tumour necrosis factor also has roles in healing.","k":"FGF mobilises fibroblasts.","rn":[["","What does FGF stand for?","What does FGF stand for?","Fibroblast growth factor"]]},{"id":45112,"t":"Tissue Repair","s":"In forming a new vessel, what is the role of matrix metalloproteinases?","o":["They degrade the basement membrane so the sprout can grow out","They detach pericytes by directly binding angiopoietin","They recruit leukocytes to the tip of the new sprout","They form the open lumen of the mature new vessel","They convert fibrinogen to fibrin in the new vessel"],"e":"Matrix metalloproteinases (MMPs) are responsible for basement membrane degradation in the first stage of angiogenesis. This lets the tip cell push outward. Pericyte detachment is mediated by angiopoietin and vasodilation by vascular endothelial growth factor. Fibrosis signalling also includes matrix metalloproteinases from macrophages.","w":["Pericyte detachment is mediated by angiopoietin.","Recruiting leukocytes is not their role here.","The open lumen results from later stages of formation.","Fibrin formation follows the coagulation cascade."],"k":"MMPs degrade basement membrane in angiogenesis.","rn":[["","What degrades the basement membrane in angiogenesis?","What degrades the basement membrane in angiogenesis?","MMPs"],["","What happens to pericytes and basement membrane at the start of angiogenesis?","What happens to pericytes and basement membrane at the start of angiogenesis?","Pericytes detach (angiopoietin); Basement membrane degrades (MMPs)"]]},{"id":45113,"t":"Tissue Repair","s":"What is a leading tip cell in angiogenesis, and which signals act on it?","o":["An endothelial cell pushing outward under VEGF and Notch signals","A pericyte moving inward under angiopoietin and collagen signals","A macrophage engulfing debris under phosphatidylserine signals","A neutrophil rolling along selectins under chemokine signals","A hepatocyte moving from G0 to G1 under interleukin 6 signals"],"e":"In the first stage, a leading tip cell pushes outward from the quiescent vessel. The relevant signals are vascular endothelial growth factor and Notch. Vascular endothelial cells drive the formation of the new vessel through proliferation. The stalk then elongates behind the tip cell.","w":["Pericytes detach from the vessel rather than leading the sprout.","A macrophage is not the tip cell of a vascular sprout.","A rolling neutrophil is not the tip cell of a vascular sprout.","Hepatocyte priming is part of liver regeneration."],"k":"Tip cell: endothelial, driven by VEGF and Notch.","rn":[["","Which signals drive the leading tip cell?","Which signals drive the leading tip cell?","VEGF; Notch"]]},{"id":45114,"t":"Tissue Regeneration vs Repair","s":"A tissue section of intestinal epithelium, in which marked stem-cell-derived tissue is blue, is followed at 1, 5 and 60 days. What pattern shows healing from stem cells?","o":["A tiny blue spot at day 1 grows from the base, running along the structure by day 60","A wholly blue surface at day 1 fades to no blue at all by day 60","Blue appears first at the luminal tip at day 60 and then vanishes by day 1","The blue area is unchanged at each time point, as stem cells stay asleep","Blue appears only in the muscular layer and never reaches the epithelium"],"e":"At 1 day the tissue is mostly red with a tiny spot of blue, the start of healing from stem cells. At 5 days the blue area has grown up from the base. At 60 days, blue runs along the structure and regeneration is pretty much complete. This shows quiescent stem cells waking to rebuild.","w":["The blue area grows rather than fades.","The time sequence of this option is backwards.","Stem cells wake in response to signalling, so the blue area changes.","The signal is in the epithelial structure."],"k":"Intestinal stem cell progeny expand from base over 60 days."},{"id":45115,"t":"Tissue Regeneration vs Repair","s":"Two patients have the same injury. In patient A, macrophages clear debris rapidly with good vascular drainage. In patient B, debris and fluid remain. Which prediction is best?","o":["A is more likely to resolve; B is more likely to have prolonged inflammation","A is more likely to have prolonged inflammation; B is more likely to resolve","Both are equally likely to resolve, as debris and fluid in the wound do not affect resolution in any way","Both must form keloids, as any debris clearance produces too much collagen","Neither can heal, because macrophages are irrelevant to wound healing"],"e":"Quick removal of cell debris by macrophage phagocytosis and fluids by good vascular drainage is a condition for resolution. Without it, the conditions for resolution are not satisfied. If inflammation does not end, wound healing runs into problems. So patient A has the better outlook.","w":["This reverses the two outcomes.","Debris and fluid removal is a listed condition.","Keloids are excessive scars and do not result from debris clearance.","Macrophages are the essential cells in this process."],"k":"Debris and fluid clearance is required for resolution.","rn":[["","Which injuries push regenerative tissue into repair?","Which injuries push regenerative tissue into repair?","Very severe injury; Prolonged injury (chronic inflammation)"]]},{"id":45116,"t":"Tissue Repair","s":"A patient has a single mild toxic injury to the liver with loss of hepatocytes but intact architecture. What is the expected outcome?","o":["Recovery, because hepatocytes are stable cells and architecture is intact","Permanent loss, because hepatocytes are terminally differentiated cells that cannot divide","Cirrhosis, because any hepatocyte loss must always lead to fibrous bands","Keloid formation, because the liver forms scar beyond its normal boundary","Callus formation, because the liver repairs in the same manner as bone"],"e":"Hepatocytes are stable cells with good regenerative capacity. Hepatocyte loss with intact architecture allows recovery. When damage destroys architecture, full regeneration is not possible and cirrhosis can arise. So the state of the architecture, not merely cell loss, predicts the outcome.","w":["Hepatocytes are stable, not permanent.","Hepatocyte loss alone does not always lead to cirrhosis.","A keloid is a skin scar disorder.","Callus is a stage of fracture healing."],"k":"Hepatocyte loss with intact architecture can recover.","rn":[["","Hepatocyte loss with architecture intact: what outcome?","Hepatocyte loss with architecture intact: what outcome?","Recovery possible"]]},{"id":45117,"t":"Tissue Repair","s":"Which macrophage-related population is labelled as residing in bone, and what do macrophages do in the early stage of fracture repair?","o":["Osteoclasts; removal of necrotic tissue and debris","Osteoclasts; producing the initial haematoma","Kupffer cells; removing necrotic tissue and debris","Mesangial phagocytes; forming the lamellar bone","Microglial cells; forming the callus around the bone"],"e":"Osteoclasts are the population labelled in bone. Early in fracture repair, a haematoma forms immediately, and then macrophages clear the debris by removing necrotic tissue. Callus and lamellar bone follow later. Local macrophage names vary by tissue.","w":["A haematoma is coagulated blood, not produced by osteoclasts.","Kupffer cells are in the liver.","Mesangial phagocytes are in the kidney.","Microglial cells are in the brain."],"k":"Bone: osteoclasts; macrophages clear debris after the haematoma.","rn":[["","What happens to necrotic tissue in bone repair?","What happens to necrotic tissue in bone repair?","It is removed"],["","What can tissue resident macrophages clear?","What can tissue resident macrophages clear?","Infectious pathogens; Cell debris after cell death"],["","What does scar formation follow?","What does scar formation follow?","Resolution of inflammation; Removal of immune cell debris"]]},{"id":45118,"t":"Tissue Regeneration vs Repair","s":"What most determines whether healing occurs by regeneration or by repair?","o":["The type of injured tissue and the nature of the wound","The patient's height and the colour of the skin","Whether M1 macrophages exist at all in the patient","The presence of phosphatidylserine in the healthy cells","The specific antibiotic used for treatment of the wound"],"e":"Regeneration versus repair depends on the type of injured tissue and the nature of the wound. Some tissues have more regenerative capacity than others, with adult stem cells as the key. Superficial, small wounds favour regeneration, while severe or prolonged injuries favour repair.","w":["These features are not given as determinants.","M1 macrophages are not the deciding factor.","Healthy cells always have phosphatidylserine on the inside of the membrane.","The antibiotic used is not the main determinant."],"k":"Tissue type and wound nature decide regeneration vs repair.","rn":[["","Regeneration or repair depends on which 2 things?","Regeneration or repair depends on which 2 things?","Type of injured tissue; Nature of the wound"]]},{"id":45119,"t":"Scar Formation","s":"What fills the gap in repair, as opposed to regeneration?","a":["connective tissue"],"e":"In repair, connective tissue rather than the original tissue fills the gap, described as patching up, and a scar forms. In regeneration, the original tissue fills the gap and the tissue returns to normal. This is why repair restores only some function.","k":"Repair fills the gap with connective tissue.","rn":[["","What is meant by Scar Formation?","What is meant by Scar Formation?","Repair by depositing connective tissue"],["","What is tissue repair characterised by?","What is tissue repair characterised by?","Scar formation; Deposition of connective tissue"],["","Why is bone a good candidate for repair?","Why is bone a good candidate for repair?","Bone is connective tissue; Repair deposits connective tissue"]]},{"id":45120,"t":"Problems in Wound Healing","s":"Why is rapid covering of an open wound prioritised even at the cost of perfect tissue restoration?","o":["An open wound is an opportunity for microbes to enter the body","An open wound stops all circulation in the affected limb","An open wound causes keloids unless it is covered in an hour","An open wound makes all neighbouring cells become permanent","An open wound prevents macrophages from ever differentiating"],"e":"An open wound is an opportunity for microbes to enter the body, so it needs to be covered as soon as possible. This helps explain repair and the first non-specific collagen, whose aim is fast cover. Repair sacrifices some function to close the breach.","w":["Stopping all circulation is not the reason given.","A keloid is not caused by delayed covering within an hour.","Conversion of neighbouring cells to permanent cells is not described.","Macrophages differentiate from monocytes in tissues."],"k":"Open wounds admit microbes, so cover comes first.","rn":[["","Which 3 wound contents impair healing locally?","Which 3 wound contents impair healing locally?","Foreign body; Haematoma; Necrotic tissue"]]},{"id":45121,"t":"Resolution of Inflammation","s":"A 35-year-old has a skin wound in which many dying cells are present. A research biopsy shows that the dying cells have phosphatidylserine on the outside of their membranes, but the macrophages have a defect in their receptors for this lipid. Debris stays in the wound for weeks, and vessels remain dilated. The wound fails to progress to rebuilding.<br><br>What is the most direct explanation for the failure to heal?","o":["Failed recognition blocks engulfment, so debris remains and resolution cannot occur","Failed \"don't eat me\" signalling makes macrophages eat all the healthy neighbouring cells","Failed phosphatidylserine flipping keeps the dying cells invisible from the very start","Failed lysophosphatidic acid release stops phagocytes from even reaching the wound area","Failed collagen deposition by the macrophages leaves the wound without any scar tissue"],"e":"Step 1: phosphatidylserine has flipped normally, so the dying cells carry the signal, but macrophages cannot recognise and bind them. Without recognition and binding, signalling and phagocytosis do not follow. Step 2: quick removal of debris and the end of vasodilation are conditions for resolution, and inflammation must end for healing to work. So the wound stalls at this stage.","w":["The defect is in phosphatidylserine receptors rather than \"don't eat me\" signalling.","Flipping is normal here, as the biopsy shows it on the outside of the membrane.","The macrophages are present in the wound, and the problem lies with their receptors.","Fibroblasts deposit collagen, not macrophages."],"k":"PtdSer receptor defect → no engulfment → no resolution.","rn":[["","What are the 4 steps of debris phagocytosis?","What are the 4 steps of debris phagocytosis?","Recognition; Binding; Signalling; Phagocytosis"],["","What does scar formation follow?","What does scar formation follow?","Resolution of inflammation; Removal of immune cell debris"],["","What are the 5 conditions for resolution to proceed?","What are the 5 conditions for resolution to proceed?","Minimal cell death, tissue damage; Regenerative capacity of tissue; Quick destruction of pathogens; Quick removal of debris, fluids; End of vasodilation"]]},{"id":45122,"t":"Macrophages and Growth Factors","s":"A 60-year-old has a large wound that has been free of infection for days. Debris is cleared, but fibroblasts, endothelial cells and epithelial cells are barely proliferating. Pro-inflammatory cytokines remain high, and growth factor levels remain low. Macrophages are abundant in the wound.<br><br>Which macrophage imbalance best explains this, and what is its direct consequence?","o":["Too few M2 macrophages, so growth factors do not drive proliferation","Too few M1 macrophages, so debris could not be phagocytosed or digested","Too many M2 macrophages, so growth factors overstimulate every target cell","Too few monocytes in blood, so no macrophages have reached the wound site","Too many tissue-resident macrophages, so all stem cells have been consumed"],"e":"Step 1: high pro-inflammatory cytokines with low growth factors suggest M1-type activity without a shift to M2 macrophages, which release anti-inflammatory cytokines and growth factors. Step 2: growth factors act on epithelial cells, fibroblasts, endothelial cells and stem cells, altering gene expression to drive proliferation and differentiation. Without them, the rebuilding stage stalls.","w":["The debris has been cleared, and pro-inflammatory cytokines are high.","Too many M2 macrophages would raise growth factors rather than lower them.","Macrophages are abundant, so delivery is not the problem.","Macrophages do not consume stem cells."],"k":"Low M2 activity → low growth factors → stalled proliferation.","rn":[["","What do M2 macrophages release?","What do M2 macrophages release?","Anti-inflammatory cytokines; Growth factors"]]},{"id":45123,"t":"Tissue Regeneration vs Repair","s":"A 50-year-old donates part of their liver. The remaining liver architecture is normal. Two weeks after surgery, the liver has grown considerably, but in an experimental model a similar liver keeps enlarging. In the model, hepatocytes continue to divide after the original mass is reached, though priming and growth factor signals were normal at the start.<br><br>Which part of the process has failed in the model, and what would it normally do?","o":["Anti-proliferative cytokine release, which normally returns hepatocytes to quiescence","Interleukin 6 release from Kupffer cells, which normally ends hepatocyte proliferation","Hepatocyte growth factor binding to MET, which normally returns cells to the G0 phase","Tumour necrosis factor signalling, which normally destroys any excess hepatocytes","Epidermal growth factor binding to EGFR, which normally forms the fibrous scar bands"],"e":"Step 1: intact architecture and normal initial signals explain the regeneration through priming by tumour necrosis factor and interleukin 6, followed by growth factor-driven proliferation. Step 2: the failure is in stopping. Once liver mass is restored, anti-proliferative cytokines are normally released and hepatocytes return to quiescence, without which the liver keeps growing.","w":["Interleukin 6 is part of priming, not stopping.","Hepatocyte growth factor drives proliferation rather than stopping it.","Tumour necrosis factor acts in priming through Kupffer cells.","Epidermal growth factor drives proliferation rather than scar formation."],"k":"Anti-proliferative cytokines stop liver regeneration.","rn":[["","How does liver regeneration end?","How does liver regeneration end?","Anti-proliferative cytokines released; Hepatocytes return to quiescence"]]},{"id":45124,"t":"Problems in Wound Healing","s":"A 58-year-old has had repeated liver injury for many years. Imaging and biopsy show a small lumpy liver with fibrous bands and regenerative nodules. Hepatocytes are present and some are proliferating. However, liver function has declined steadily.<br><br>Why have surviving hepatocytes not restored the organ, and what is the functional result?","o":["Destroyed architecture prevents full regeneration, so scarring causes loss of organ function","Hepatocytes are permanent cells that cannot divide, so the nodules must be made of fibroblasts","Kupffer cells are absent from all cirrhotic livers, so there is no longer any priming signal","Fibrous bands regenerate into hepatocytes over time, so function should soon return to normal","Anti-proliferative cytokines cannot be released in this liver, so regeneration is complete"],"e":"Step 1: hepatocytes are stable cells with good regenerative capacity, but when damage destroys liver architecture, full regeneration is not possible. Fibrous scarring and rounded regenerative nodules replace the normal units, which is cirrhosis. Step 2: excessive fibrosis in a high-functioning organ means the scarred parts can no longer do their normal job, so organ function is lost.","w":["Hepatocytes are stable cells, and the patient has proliferating cells.","Absence of Kupffer cells is not the stated reason.","Fibrous scar does not turn back into hepatocytes.","A failure to stop would not explain the destroyed architecture and fibrosis."],"k":"Destroyed liver architecture → cirrhosis → loss of function.","rn":[["","What can excessive tissue fibrosis lead to?","What can excessive tissue fibrosis lead to?","Fibrotic disorders; Loss of organ function"]]},{"id":45125,"t":"Tissue Repair","s":"A 28-year-old sustains a tibial fracture. A few days later, imaging shows a clot at the site with debris being cleared. Some weeks later there is irregular material bridging the break. The patient then rests the limb and refuses all advised movement for months. The bone remains structurally poor.<br><br>Which stage had been reached at the \"weeks\" scan, and which later stage would inactivity most directly hinder?","o":["Callus had formed; remodelling would be hindered because it depends on motion","Haematoma had formed; callus would be hindered because it depends on motion","Lamellar bone had formed; haematoma would be hindered because it depends on motion","Remodelling had finished; debris removal would be hindered by the lack of motion","Keloid had formed; lamellar bone would be hindered because the keloid is excessive"],"e":"Step 1: the irregular bridging material is the callus, the first new bone, with unstructured bone and cartilage, following the haematoma and removal of necrotic tissue. Step 2: more structural lamellar bone replaces woven bone, and remodelling depends on the mechanical pressures of motion. So avoiding movement directly hinders remodelling, which is why following advice in recovery matters.","w":["The clot was seen days earlier, so bridging material is not a haematoma.","Lamellar replacement comes after callus, and a haematoma forms immediately.","Remodelling is the last stage and had not been completed.","A keloid is a skin scar disorder."],"k":"Irregular bridge = callus; remodelling needs motion.","rn":[["","What does bone remodelling depend on?","What does bone remodelling depend on?","Mechanical pressures of motion"]]},{"id":45126,"t":"Scar Formation","s":"A 45-year-old has a large infected wound with tissue loss, which cannot be closed. Two weeks later, its base is red and bumpy with a sticky surface. A biopsy with a collagen stain shows fine threads of collagen among capillary loops. Macrophages have already cleared most of the debris.<br><br>Which stage has been reached, and what is expected to happen next?","o":["Granulation; fibroblasts deposit matrix, then the epidermis grows over and remodelling follows","Remodelling; the type I scar is being degraded so that the original tissue can return completely over the months ahead","Regeneration; stem cells fill the gap with original tissue, so no scar will form at all","First intention; the weak fibrin join will become a strong collagen join within a few days","Keloid; the excess collagen will spread far beyond the boundary of this wound in time"],"e":"Step 1: a red, bumpy, sticky surface with capillary loops and sparse collagen indicates granulation tissue in second intention healing, after phagocytosis. Step 2: fibroblasts migrate, proliferate and deposit extracellular matrix, then the connective tissue supports epidermal growth across the top. Remodelling then gives scar contraction. The outcome will be a scar with at most 80% of normal skin strength.","w":["Sparse collagen and abundant loops are too early for remodelling, and original tissue does not return.","Regeneration is not possible where tissue is lost and the sides cannot align.","First intention needs a minor wound with aligned sides.","A keloid has excessive collagen, not sparse threads."],"k":"Granulation → matrix → epithelium → remodelling.","rn":[["","Which cells deposit collagen in a scar?","Which cells deposit collagen in a scar?","Fibroblasts"],["","What structure is remodelled in the remodelling phase?","What structure is remodelled in the remodelling phase?","Extracellular matrix"],["","Second intention: what stage follows granulation tissue?","Second intention: what stage follows granulation tissue?","Organisation"]]},{"id":45127,"t":"Scar Formation","s":"A 24-year-old has a small wound on the shoulder that heals, but over a year the scar keeps growing. It now extends well past the original wound and stands out from the skin. A biopsy shows thick pink bands of collagen in the dermis. A relative has had a similar problem.<br><br>What is the diagnosis, and how is it distinguished from the closest alternative?","o":["Keloid, because it passes beyond the boundary, unlike a hypertrophic scar along the wound line","Hypertrophic scar, because it passes beyond the boundary, unlike a keloid along the wound line","Chronic ulcer, because it has lost tissue, unlike a keloid that has excess collagen in the dermis","Granulation tissue, because it is growing, unlike a mature scar that has mostly type I collagen","Normal remodelling, because scars always expand with time, unlike any hypertrophic scar"],"e":"Step 1: the scar grows beyond the original boundary, does not regress and shows excessive dermal collagen, which indicates a keloid. A possible genetic predisposition may fit the family history, though this is not quite clear. Step 2: a hypertrophic scar also has excessive collagen but stays along the line of the wound. So the discriminator is whether the scar has passed the boundary.","w":["This reverses the two definitions.","An ulcer is loss of tissue, whereas this lesion protrudes.","Granulation tissue is red and sticky with sparse collagen.","Normal remodelling makes the scar contract rather than expand."],"k":"Keloid passes the wound boundary; hypertrophic scar does not.","rn":[["","What is meant by Keloid?","What is meant by Keloid?","Scar grows beyond its boundaries; Doesn't regress"],["","Image card: What is this scar? How does it behave?","What is this scar? How does it behave?","Keloid; Grows beyond boundaries, doesn't regress"]]},{"id":45128,"t":"Problems in Wound Healing","s":"A 72-year-old with diabetes mellitus and poor nutrition has a foot ulcer with a dark necrotic centre on the sole. The foot has poor blood supply, and the wound contains necrotic tissue. Fibroblast activity and white cell function are both poor. Previous wounds in this patient have also healed slowly.<br><br>Which option correctly sorts the factors, and explains why the ulcer persists?","o":["Diabetes, age and nutrition are systemic; poor perfusion and necrosis are local; all reduce healing","Diabetes, age and nutrition are local; poor perfusion and necrosis are systemic; all reduce healing","All of the factors are local, and only the necrotic centre of the ulcer is stopping the healing process from going on","All of the factors are systemic, and only the patient's age is stopping any of the healing","None of the factors matters, because ulcers are simply excessive scars that always keep growing"],"e":"Step 1: age, diabetes mellitus (impairing leucocyte function) and poor nutrition (no resources) are systemic factors. Poor perfusion (less delivery of cells, growth factors and resources) and necrotic tissue are local factors. Step 2: the ulcer is a chronic wound with tissue necrosis and failure to heal, and these combined factors mean the wound cannot progress.","w":["This reverses the two groups.","Diabetes, age and nutrition act from the whole body.","Poor perfusion and necrotic tissue act at the wound.","An ulcer is a loss of tissue with failure to heal, not an excessive scar."],"k":"Ulcers: combined local and systemic impairment of healing.","rn":[["","Which 4 supply and defence factors impair healing locally?","Which 4 supply and defence factors impair healing locally?","Local infection; Low white blood cell counts; Poor blood supply or perfusion; Denervation"],["","Which 4 patient factors impair healing systemically?","Which 4 patient factors impair healing systemically?","Age; Obesity; Diabetes mellitus; Soft tissue genetic disorders"]]},{"id":45129,"t":"Tissue Repair","s":"A 40-year-old has a healing wound in which new vessel formation is being studied. In the wound's vessels, endothelial cells are dividing and tip cells have formed in response to signals, but the basement membrane around the vessel stays intact. Few sprouts leave the vessel, and granulation tissue is thin.<br><br>Which molecule's action is most likely deficient, and what is the consequence for healing?","o":["Matrix metalloproteinases; sprouts fail to leave, so granulation tissue and repair are poor","Angiopoietin; pericytes detach too strongly, so sprouts form a complete new lumen","Vascular endothelial growth factor; dilation is excessive, so the wound heals very quickly","Phagocyte oxidase; hypochlorite is lacking, so all new vessels are destroyed by microbes","Lysophosphatidic acid; macrophages fail to arrive, so the basement membrane must stay intact"],"e":"Step 1: in the first stage of angiogenesis, vasodilation involves vascular endothelial growth factor, pericyte detachment involves angiopoietin, and basement membrane degradation involves matrix metalloproteinases. An intact membrane therefore points to deficient matrix metalloproteinases. Step 2: without sprouting there are few new vessels, and granulation tissue relies on angiogenesis, so repair and delivery of resources suffer.","w":["Excess detachment would not leave the basement membrane intact or form a complete lumen.","The membrane is intact, and a wound with this defect would not heal very quickly.","This chemistry kills microbes and is not the step that degrades the membrane.","Attracting phagocytes is not the mechanism that degrades the basement membrane."],"k":"MMP deficiency → intact membrane → failed sprouting.","rn":[["","What happens after pericyte detachment in angiogenesis?","What happens after pericyte detachment in angiogenesis?","Pericytes are recruited; ECM (extracellular matrix) forms between sprouts"]]},{"id":45130,"t":"Tissue Regeneration vs Repair","s":"Two 30-year-olds have skin injuries. Patient A has a clean, thin scalpel incision with sides close together. Patient B has a wide injury with tissue loss and infection, and the sides cannot be brought together. Both have normal nutrition, and neither takes any regular medicines.<br><br>Which option correctly predicts the wound's key tissue and final result for each?","o":["A: fibrin then collagen join, near-normal; B: granulation then scar, at most 80% strength","A: granulation then scar, at most 80% strength; B: fibrin then collagen join, near-normal","A: granulation then keloid in all cases; B: fibrin then full regeneration in all cases","A: callus then lamellar replacement of woven bone; B: haematoma then a normal lamellar bone remodelling process","A: no healing without antibiotics; B: complete regeneration through stem cells alone"],"e":"Step 1: patient A has a superficial, aligned, clean cut, so healing is by first intention, with a weak fibrin join followed by a strong collagen join and epidermal regrowth. Step 2: patient B has lost tissue and the sides cannot align, so healing goes via repair by second intention, with granulation tissue, then scar, remodelling and contraction. A scar reaches at most 80% of normal skin strength.","w":["This reverses the two routes.","A keloid is not expected in all cases, and full regeneration is not possible with B's tissue loss.","Callus and lamellar bone are stages in fracture healing.","First intention heals without needing antibiotics, and B cannot regenerate completely."],"k":"Alignment and tissue loss determine first vs second intention.","rn":[["","What are the 3 steps of first intention healing?","What are the 3 steps of first intention healing?","Exudation of fibrinogen; Weak fibrin join; Strong collagen join, epidermal regrowth"],["","What is the maximum strength of a scar compared with normal skin?","What is the maximum strength of a scar compared with normal skin?","80%"]]}]);
